/* GENERATED by tools/import_book.js from the finished manuscript of Book 3. Story text is the author's, verbatim; do not edit here. */
(function(){ G.HFQ.addSeriesText("htfa-3",{"chapters":[{"t":"Prologue — The Color of the Morning","blocks":[{"p":"The first thing I noticed about the sky was that I couldn’t see much of it."},{"p":"Not properly, anyway. The kitchen window had turned into a gray rectangle, and the neighbor’s basketball hoop looked like somebody had rubbed an eraser over half of it. Beyond the houses, a lawn mower started, coughed, and gave up."},{"p":"Dad stood at the counter trying to scrape peanut butter out of a nearly empty jar with a butter knife. He had his work badge on backward. Mom had stopped mentioning it months ago."},{"p":"“You going to eat that?” he asked, pointing the knife at my toast."},{"p":"“Eventually.”"},{"p":"“A bold plan.”"},{"p":"My phone was open beside my plate. The weather app showed a cheerful yellow sun over Virginia Beach."},{"p":"I turned the screen toward him."},{"p":"Dad looked out the window, then back at the phone. “Maybe the sun hasn’t checked in yet.”"},{"p":"“It says clear.”"},{"p":"“The toast says you’re eating breakfast. Both of you are making promises.”"},{"p":"I was fifteen now. I had been fifteen for twelve days, which apparently meant I was old enough to notice when a computer was wrong but not old enough to remember to bring my laundry downstairs. Mom had explained this distinction twice already."},{"p":"I refreshed the app. The sun refused to leave."},{"p":"A message arrived from Imani."},{"p":"*Can you see the water tower from your street?*"},{"p":"I stepped onto the porch in my socks. The boards were damp, and the air felt like the inside of a cold glass. At the end of the block, where the tower usually rose above the roofs, there was only more gray."},{"p":"*No,* I wrote."},{"p":"*Same here. My dad says the highway signs are disappearing until he’s right under them.*"},{"p":"*My app says sunshine.*"},{"p":"*Your app lives somewhere else.*"},{"p":"I hated how good that answer was."},{"p":"We weren’t flying that morning. The academy didn’t meet until Saturday, and it was Wednesday. But the fog bothered me because I couldn’t find a clean edge to it. Clouds usually had shapes. Rain had a beginning and an end, even if you got soaked between them. This was air that looked solid without being solid at all."},{"p":"At the bus stop, my friend Theo Marshall emerged from the gray carrying his backpack on one shoulder."},{"p":"“You look like you’re waiting for a ghost,” he said."},{"p":"“I’m looking at the visibility.”"},{"p":"“The what?”"},{"p":"“How far you can see.”"},{"p":"Theo stared at me. “Not far. You’re welcome.”"},{"p":"A car appeared at the intersection without much warning. Its headlights glowed first; the hood came later. The driver slowed well before the stop sign."},{"p":"I took another step back from the curb."},{"p":"For two summers I’d spent so much time learning what an airplane could do that I’d started to forget something important: the sky didn’t owe anybody a clear view."},{"p":"The bus arrived late, hissing to a stop with its yellow sides wet from the mist. Theo climbed aboard ahead of me."},{"p":"“You coming?”"},{"p":"I took one last look at the place where the water tower should have been."},{"p":"“Yeah,” I said, and followed him in."}]},{"t":"Chapter One — The Window Nobody Trusted","blocks":[{"p":"By Saturday, the fog was gone. In its place we had a hot blue sky, the kind that made the parking lot at Hampton Roads Executive Airport shimmer before noon."},{"p":"Rafe was sitting on the low wall outside the academy classroom, eating something from a foil wrapper."},{"p":"“Please tell me that’s not breakfast,” I said."},{"p":"“It’s a breakfast burrito.”"},{"p":"“It smells like a tire fire.”"},{"p":"“That’s the sausage.”"},{"p":"He offered me a bite. I declined. He looked wounded."},{"p":"Inside, Beckett had taken apart a cheap desk fan. Its grille and screws were arranged in neat rows on a towel. Imani was standing over him with her arms folded."},{"p":"“You’re supposed to be setting up the projector,” she said."},{"p":"“I am.”"},{"p":"“The projector is over there.”"},{"p":"“It overheats. I’m improving airflow.”"},{"p":"“By removing airflow from a different machine?”"},{"p":"Beckett looked at the fan parts. “When you say it like that, it sounds less helpful.”"},{"p":"I put my bag under a chair. The room smelled of dry-erase markers and warm plastic. Through the window, a Cessna taxied toward the runway, its white wings bright enough to make me squint."},{"p":"Mara Ellery entered with a stack of printouts and an ordinary kitchen sponge. She set both on the table."},{"p":"“Morning,” she said. “Nobody touch the sponge.”"},{"p":"Rafe raised his hand. “Is the sponge dangerous?”"},{"p":"“Only if you eat it.”"},{"p":"“So less dangerous than my burrito,” I said."},{"p":"“Depends on the burrito.”"},{"p":"Mara taped three photographs to the board. In the first, a runway stretched toward a distant tree line beneath a clear sky. In the second, the same runway vanished into pale haze. In the third, the pavement was wet and a low bank of cloud hung beyond the far end."},{"p":"“Which picture would you choose for your first flight of the day?” she asked."},{"p":"“One,” I said."},{"p":"“Why?”"},{"p":"“You can see farther.”"},{"p":"“Probably. What else?”"},{"p":"I looked again. The photos didn’t show wind, temperature, or what was happening beyond the trees. They didn’t tell us when they’d been taken."},{"p":"“We don’t know enough,” I said."},{"p":"Mara nodded once. “That’s a better answer.”"},{"p":"Beckett leaned toward me. “You just got credit for not answering.”"},{"p":"“A skill I’ve worked hard to develop.”"},{"p":"Mara passed out copies of a routine aviation weather report, a METAR, and a forecast, a TAF. The pages looked like somebody had spilled alphabet soup across a keyboard."},{"p":"“There are plenty of weather products,” she said. “Today we’re starting with two you’ll see often. A METAR reports observed conditions at a particular airport and time. A TAF forecasts conditions for an airport during a specified period. Neither is a magic window into the whole sky.”"},{"p":"I found the word *visibility* on the handout and thought of the missing water tower."},{"p":"“What if a weather app says it’s sunny and you can’t see down the street?” I asked."},{"p":"“Which app? What location? What time was the observation made? Is it reporting current conditions or a forecast?” Mara asked."},{"p":"“You could just say the app is wrong.”"},{"p":"“Sometimes it is. But the interesting question is why it disagrees with what you’re seeing.”"},{"p":"Imani gave me a look that said she’d been waiting all week for somebody else to make that point."},{"p":"Mara drew a simple box around the visibility group on the sample report. She showed us how to read the observation time and explained that visibility in aviation reports has a specific meaning. A number wasn’t a guarantee that every direction looked the same or that the view would remain unchanged."},{"p":"“Pilots also care about ceilings,” she said, pointing to the cloud groups. “In this context, a ceiling is the height above the ground of the lowest cloud layer reported as broken or overcast, or vertical visibility into an obscuration.”"},{"p":"“So a cloud ceiling isn’t where clouds end?” Rafe asked."},{"p":"“Correct. It’s not the top of the clouds.”"},{"p":"“That’s badly named,” he said."},{"p":"“You can write a complaint to the sky.”"},{"p":"Mara handed me the sponge. It was dry and stiff, with one corner missing."},{"p":"“What do you think happens when I put water in this?”"},{"p":"“It gets wet,” I said."},{"p":"“Excellent. Aviation education is working.”"},{"p":"She filled a clear bowl halfway and pressed the sponge into it. Bubbles rose through the water. When she lifted it, drops fell onto the table, and Rafe scooted his papers away."},{"p":"“This is only a model,” Mara said. “Air isn’t a sponge. Water vapor is in the air, and when air cools to its dew point, some of that vapor can condense into tiny droplets. Fog is a cloud at ground level.”"},{"p":"I watched a bead of water creep down the side of the bowl."},{"p":"“So that morning at my house,” I said, “the air got cold enough for the water to show up?”"},{"p":"“That may have been part of it. There are different ways fog forms. We’d need the actual observations and conditions to explain that morning confidently.”"},{"p":"I suspected Mara had *we’d need more information* printed on a T-shirt."},{"p":"We spent the next half hour working through three imaginary flight mornings. One was sunny but windy. Another had good visibility beneath a low cloud layer. The last had improving conditions in a forecast but poor observations at the moment."},{"p":"I chose the last one for a flight in the afternoon."},{"p":"“You haven’t been given an afternoon observation,” Mara said."},{"p":"“But the forecast improves.”"},{"p":"“Maybe it will. Are you deciding to fly now, or deciding to check again later?”"},{"p":"I crossed out my answer and wrote: *Check again later. No departure decision yet.*"},{"p":"Beckett glanced over. “That’s a lot of words for ‘maybe.’”"},{"p":"“It’s an educated maybe.”"},{"p":"“The worst kind.”"},{"p":"At lunch we sat outside on the low wall, our food balanced on our knees. A small airplane climbed out beyond the hangars. Its engine note faded while the airplane was still easy to see."},{"p":"Imani took a folded paper from her pocket. It was a printout about the Steven F. Udvar-Hazy Center in Chantilly. My family was planning a trip there later that summer, and we’d spent weeks trying to find a weekend when the others could come along."},{"p":"“They have the Space Shuttle Discovery,” she said, although everybody already knew that because she’d told us four times."},{"p":"“They also have the SR-71,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"“Just making sure.”"},{"p":"Rafe pointed at the schedule with the corner of his burrito wrapper. “I’m going to find the biggest airplane and stand under it until someone takes my picture.”"},{"p":"“You can’t stand under every airplane,” I said."},{"p":"“Not with that attitude.”"},{"p":"Imani smiled, but only briefly. She had been quieter than usual, checking her phone between conversations."},{"p":"“Everything okay?” I asked."},{"p":"“Yeah. My grandmother’s appointment got moved again. Mom’s trying to sort out rides.”"},{"p":"“Do you need—”"},{"p":"“I don’t know yet,” she said, a little too fast. Then she looked embarrassed. “Sorry. It’s just a lot of messages.”"},{"p":"I nodded. A year earlier I would have offered a solution before she’d finished explaining the problem. I still wanted to. Instead I picked at the label on my water bottle until it came off in a long damp strip."},{"p":"Across the ramp, a mechanic rolled a toolbox toward an open hangar. Somewhere inside, metal struck metal twice."},{"p":"“You can tell us when you know,” I said."},{"p":"Imani tucked her phone away. “Thanks.”"},{"p":"Rafe looked at the empty wrapper in his hand. “Speaking of important problems, I think I need another burrito.”"},{"p":"“You need a doctor,” Beckett said."},{"p":"Imani laughed—really laughed—for the first time all morning."}]},{"t":"Chapter Two — The Picture with No Date","blocks":[{"p":"On Monday afternoon, Theo and I got caught in a thunderstorm on the way home from the library."},{"p":"There was no movie-style lightning strike beside us. Just three blocks between us and my house, no umbrellas, and a rain cloud that seemed personally offended by our existence."},{"p":"The first drops were big enough to leave dark circles on the sidewalk. Then the street went silver."},{"p":"“Run!” Theo shouted."},{"p":"I ran. My backpack slapped against my shoulders, and my sneakers made wet squeaks on the pavement. We reached the covered entrance of a closed dentist’s office just as the rain became too heavy to see across the parking lot."},{"p":"Theo bent over with his hands on his knees. “That was stupid.”"},{"p":"“Which part?”"},{"p":"“The part where we didn’t check the weather.”"},{"p":"“I checked this morning.”"},{"p":"“And?”"},{"p":"“Twenty percent chance of rain.”"},{"p":"He looked out at the water hammering the roof of a parked minivan. “That twenty percent is giving a hundred percent.”"},{"p":"Thunder rolled above the buildings. The awning kept off the rain, but it wasn’t safe shelter from lightning. The office was closed, and we couldn’t get inside, so we stayed away from the open lot while I texted Mom our location and asked for a ride."},{"p":"Theo held up his phone. “My dad says a storm cell’s moving east.”"},{"p":"“Where’d he see that?”"},{"p":"“Radar.”"},{"p":"I opened a weather radar image on my own phone. A patch of red and yellow sat over the city. It looked alarming, but I didn’t know exactly when the image had been taken."},{"p":"“It says updated at three forty,” I said."},{"p":"Theo checked the time. “It’s four oh-six.”"},{"p":"I refreshed. A newer radar image appeared. The colored area had shifted east, and its shape had changed, growing a ragged tail I hadn’t noticed before."},{"p":"“You know what?” I said. “I was about to show you the first picture and tell you where the storm is now.”"},{"p":"“But it isn’t now.”"},{"p":"“Right.”"},{"p":"“You really do spend too much time at that airport.”"},{"p":"A flash lit the underside of the clouds. We stopped talking until the thunder passed."},{"p":"Mom arrived twenty minutes later. She pulled up close to the entrance and waved us over only when the lightning and heavy rain had moved farther away. We climbed into the car with our shoes squelching on the floor mats."},{"p":"“You two look like abandoned dish towels,” she said."},{"p":"“Weather happened,” Theo replied."},{"p":"“That’s generally what weather does.”"},{"p":"She dropped Theo at his house, then turned toward ours. Water ran along the gutters in narrow brown streams. At the next intersection, a branch lay across part of the road, and Mom slowed to steer around it."},{"p":"“I saw the radar,” she said. “Your message was the right call.”"},{"p":"“I thought the storm wasn’t supposed to be this bad.”"},{"p":"“Maybe it wasn’t supposed to be.”"},{"p":"I stared at the windshield wipers. Their rubber edges squeaked at the end of each stroke."},{"p":"“I hate not knowing what happens next,” I said."},{"p":"“Most people do.”"},{"p":"“Mara says forecasts aren’t promises.”"},{"p":"“She’s right.”"},{"p":"“That’s not very comforting.”"},{"p":"Mom turned into our street. “Knowing the limits of something can still help you use it well.”"},{"p":"I wanted to argue, but she was concentrating on a cyclist waiting beside a flooded curb, and the moment passed."},{"p":"At home, Dad had put an old towel by the front door. He handed me a second one and told me to leave my shoes outside. Then he asked whether the library books in my bag had survived."},{"p":"“They’re mine,” I said."},{"p":"“That wasn’t the question.”"},{"p":"My notebook was damp at the edges, but the pages weren’t ruined. I spread it open on the kitchen table, and the paper curled as it dried."},{"p":"One page held Mara’s notes from Saturday: *Observation. Forecast. Time. Location. Uncertainty.*"},{"p":"I drew a little box beside each word. Then I wrote what had happened at the dentist’s office, including the time on the radar image and the time I had actually looked at it."},{"p":"Dad leaned over my shoulder. “Homework?”"},{"p":"“Sort of.”"},{"p":"“For school?”"},{"p":"“For making fewer dumb decisions about storms.”"},{"p":"He considered that. “Useful subject.”"},{"p":"My phone buzzed. It was the academy group chat."},{"p":"Beckett had posted a photograph of a spectacular tower of white cloud, rising like a mountain over a green field."},{"p":"*Saw this near the airport,* he wrote. *Think it’s building into a thunderstorm?*"},{"p":"Rafe answered first: *That cloud has a final boss.*"},{"p":"Imani wrote: *When did you take it?*"},{"p":"A pause."},{"p":"Then Beckett: *Last August.*"},{"p":"I stared at the screen."},{"p":"*You sent us a weather photo from last year?* I typed."},{"p":"*I asked what you thought it was. Didn’t say it was today’s weather.*"},{"p":"Rafe sent six skull emojis."},{"p":"I started typing something about misleading evidence, then stopped. Beckett had a point, even if he’d made it in the most Beckett way possible."},{"p":"*Could be growing vertically,* I wrote. *But one old picture can’t tell us what happened next.*"},{"p":"Imani replied: *Or what was happening outside the frame.*"},{"p":"Beckett: *Okay, good. That’s what I was trying to show.*"},{"p":"Rafe: *Sure it was, Professor Cloud.*"},{"p":"I set the phone down and looked through the window. The storm had passed. Sunlight struck the wet fence behind our yard, and water dripped from the lowest branch of the maple tree."},{"p":"The sky was blue again, almost offensively blue."},{"p":"I took a photograph. Before sending it to the group, I checked that the time and location were right."},{"p":"*Current conditions at Burgess International Airport,* I wrote. *One runway. Mostly occupied by a lawn mower.*"},{"p":"Rafe responded with a picture of his dinner and the caption: *Severe burrito warning.*"},{"p":"I couldn’t decide which was more alarming."}]},{"t":"Chapter Three — The Wrong Kind of Blue","blocks":[{"p":"On Tuesday, Theo brought a plastic rain gauge to school and announced that his backyard had received more water than the school parking lot."},{"p":"“How much more?” I asked."},{"p":"“Enough that my dad had to move the grill.”"},{"p":"“That isn’t a measurement.”"},{"p":"“It’s a measurement of how angry he was.”"},{"p":"We were standing outside the cafeteria, waiting for the doors to open. The air smelled of wet mulch and pizza dough. A few yards away, the groundskeeper was using a leaf blower to push a puddle into a different puddle. He had been doing it for ten minutes and looked personally offended by the water."},{"p":"Theo held up his phone. He’d photographed the gauge, a clear plastic tube with crooked numbers painted on its side."},{"p":"“Two inches,” he said."},{"p":"“That’s a lot.”"},{"p":"“Three, actually. My little sister filled it with the hose to see if it worked.”"},{"p":"I stared at him."},{"p":"“Scientific method,” he said."},{"p":"“You have to tell people that part first.”"},{"p":"“Why? It ruins the surprise.”"},{"p":"I thought about Beckett’s old cloud photograph. It was starting to seem like half the people I knew were running experiments designed to make me look foolish."},{"p":"At lunch I sat with my notebook open beside my tray, drawing a rough outline of the coast. The weekend academy assignment was to compare three weather sources for the same location and time. Mara had been unusually specific about the last two words."},{"p":"My phone showed a cheerful forecast for Saturday. A different site showed a row of clouds. The aviation forecast I found for a nearby airport included abbreviations that looked like someone had dropped a keyboard down the stairs."},{"p":"Theo leaned over the table. “Why does it say BKN?”"},{"p":"“Broken cloud cover.”"},{"p":"“Like the clouds need fixing?”"},{"p":"“Like most of the sky is covered, but not all of it.”"},{"p":"“Then why doesn’t it say mostly cloudy?”"},{"p":"“Because aviation weather uses categories.”"},{"p":"“Sounds exhausting.”"},{"p":"It could be. But it was also useful. In class, Mara had explained that a ceiling wasn’t just the highest cloud somebody could spot. In aviation weather reporting, the ceiling was the height above the reporting location of the lowest broken or overcast cloud layer, or vertical visibility into an obscuration. A few scattered clouds didn’t automatically create a ceiling."},{"p":"I had copied that down carefully, then circled it because I knew I’d forget the distinction."},{"p":"Theo stabbed a fork into his macaroni. “What if the sky looks perfectly blue?”"},{"p":"“Then maybe it’s clear.”"},{"p":"“Maybe?”"},{"p":"“Could be haze. Could be stuff you can’t see from here.”"},{"p":"“You’re getting less fun every year.”"},{"p":"“That’s what my dad says.”"},{"p":"After school, I took my assignment to the back porch. The afternoon was bright and hot. From my chair, I could see one small white cloud above the neighbor’s roof. It was hard to believe weather could cause trouble on a day that looked so harmless."},{"p":"I started writing: *Conditions at my house: clear, good visibility, light wind.*"},{"p":"Then I stopped."},{"p":"I hadn’t measured the visibility. I hadn’t checked the wind. I hadn’t even walked around to the front of the house, where the trees might tell a different story. I was guessing from a chair in the shade."},{"p":"I crossed out *good visibility* and wrote *distant buildings visible; distance unknown.*"},{"p":"Mom opened the screen door with her elbow, carrying a basket of laundry."},{"p":"“Doing schoolwork?” she asked."},{"p":"“Airport work.”"},{"p":"“Should I be worried that you sound disappointed?”"},{"p":"“I’m supposed to compare weather reports, but they don’t agree.”"},{"p":"“Are they for the same place?”"},{"p":"“Mostly.”"},{"p":"“The same time?”"},{"p":"I checked. One was an hourly forecast updated that morning. One was a current observation from forty minutes ago. The third was a forecast for the entire afternoon."},{"p":"“No,” I admitted."},{"p":"Mom set the basket on the porch floor. “Then they’re not necessarily disagreeing.”"},{"p":"I hated how often adults could do that. They would ask one perfectly reasonable question and undo twenty minutes of my hard work."},{"p":"She picked up a towel that had fallen out of the basket. “I’m going to need the chair back when you’re done. Your father promised to repair it.”"},{"p":"I looked at the chair. One leg was shorter than the others, and I had wedged a folded flyer beneath it."},{"p":"“He said that last month.”"},{"p":"“Yes. We’re approaching the anniversary.”"},{"p":"My phone buzzed. Imani had sent a photo of a printed weather report with three words highlighted."},{"p":"*Can you help me with this?* she wrote."},{"p":"It was unusual enough that I sat straighter. Imani was normally the one explaining things to us."},{"p":"*Which part?*"},{"p":"*All the cloud stuff. I know what the codes mean, but I keep mixing up what matters for a ceiling.*"},{"p":"I called her instead of typing. She answered on the third ring, with a television playing quietly somewhere behind her."},{"p":"“Hey,” she said."},{"p":"“Hey. I thought you had this memorized.”"},{"p":"“Some of it. Not all of it.”"},{"p":"“That’s illegal. You’re the weather person.”"},{"p":"“I’m hanging up.”"},{"p":"“Wait. I’m kidding.”"},{"p":"She didn’t laugh, and I wished I’d started differently."},{"p":"We went through the report together. I read out the cloud layers and she sorted them into scattered, broken, and overcast. When I stumbled over the height groups, she corrected me. We both got the same answer in the end, which felt better than either of us getting there alone."},{"p":"“How’s your grandmother?” I asked when we’d finished."},{"p":"A chair scraped at her end of the call. “She’s okay. My mom’s helping her with some appointments. It’s just a lot of driving.”"},{"p":"“You need anything?”"},{"p":"“No.” A pause. “Maybe the notes from Saturday if I miss it.”"},{"p":"“Sure.”"},{"p":"“And don’t make it weird.”"},{"p":"“Wasn’t planning to.”"},{"p":"“Lincoln.”"},{"p":"“Okay. I was planning a marching band.”"},{"p":"She made a small sound that might have been a laugh. “Send the notes.”"},{"p":"After we hung up, I checked the reports again. I noticed that the aviation observation was newer now, and the cloud layer had changed. The broken layer was lower than before."},{"p":"The sky above our yard was still blue."},{"p":"I could see that blue perfectly well. What I couldn’t see was the weather twenty miles away, or what the next hour might bring."},{"p":"I took a screenshot with the timestamp showing and added it to my assignment. Then I went inside to find a ruler so I could measure the chair leg Dad had been promising to fix."},{"p":"If I couldn’t repair the weather, I could at least provide accurate data about the furniture."}]},{"t":"Chapter Four — The Chair by the Window","blocks":[{"p":"Saturday morning began with a missing car key and ended, several hours later, with a question nobody wanted to answer."},{"p":"The key belonged to Dad. He searched the kitchen counter, the couch cushions, and the pocket of a jacket he hadn’t worn since February. Mom found it in the refrigerator, next to a container of leftover pasta."},{"p":"Dad examined the key as if it had moved there by itself."},{"p":"“I remember getting milk,” he said."},{"p":"“Did you also remember parking the car?” Mom asked."},{"p":"“Apparently.”"},{"p":"We were late leaving Virginia Beach. By the time we reached the academy classroom, Beckett had already set up the weather display, Rafe had claimed the chair closest to the air conditioner, and Mara was taping a large map to the whiteboard."},{"p":"“Nice of you to join us,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“Dad refrigerated the transportation.”"},{"p":"“Understandable.”"},{"p":"Imani’s usual seat by the window was empty."},{"p":"I noticed it before I noticed the stack of printed reports on the front table. That bothered me more than I wanted it to."},{"p":"Mara glanced at the clock. “Imani won’t be here this morning. Family appointment. She asked me to tell you she’d like the worksheet.”"},{"p":"I nodded and took the seat beside the window. The glass was warm against my elbow. Beyond it, a fuel truck rolled slowly past a row of tied-down airplanes."},{"p":"Mara handed out a sheet titled *A Flight That Looks Fine.* At the top was a fictional training flight planned from our airport to another airport a short distance away. The route was simple. The airplane was available. The student had been waiting three weeks for a lesson. The current weather at the departure airport looked comfortable for visual flying."},{"p":"“This seems suspicious,” Rafe said."},{"p":"“Only because you read the title,” Mara replied."},{"p":"We were given a current observation for the departure airport, a forecast for the destination, and a weather briefing that mentioned a chance of lower clouds moving in during the afternoon. The destination forecast showed conditions getting worse near our planned arrival time."},{"p":"“Decide whether you’d recommend starting this flight,” Mara said. “Write down what else you’d want to know. You have eight minutes.”"},{"p":"I started with the current weather. Visibility was good. The wind was manageable. The ceiling was comfortably above the ground. I wrote *GO* in the corner, then hesitated."},{"p":"Mara had given us a destination forecast for a reason."},{"p":"I read it again. The lower cloud layer was expected later, but the timing wasn’t exact. There was no guarantee the weather would wait until after we arrived. Even if it did, we still needed a way to get home."},{"p":"I crossed out *GO*."},{"p":"Across the aisle, Beckett tapped his pencil against his teeth. Rafe drew a tiny airplane in the margin, then erased it when Mara walked past."},{"p":"“Time,” she said."},{"p":"She asked each of us to explain our recommendation."},{"p":"Beckett wanted more information before deciding. Rafe suggested going earlier, but admitted he’d need to check whether that actually solved the problem. I said I’d postpone the flight because the destination forecast made me uncomfortable."},{"p":"Mara nodded. “What’s uncomfortable about it?”"},{"p":"“The ceiling could come down while we’re out there.”"},{"p":"“Could. Anything else?”"},{"p":"“If it gets worse, we’d need another airport. And enough fuel. And a plan that doesn’t depend on the forecast being exactly right.”"},{"p":"“Good questions. But I want you to notice something.” She pointed to the top of the worksheet. “We haven’t given you the pilot’s experience, personal limits, or the airplane’s equipment. We haven’t discussed the full weather picture along the route. You’re allowed to say you don’t have enough information.”"},{"p":"Beckett raised his hand. “Then why did you make us decide?”"},{"p":"“Because real decisions often arrive before all the information does. The useful skill is recognizing what you’re missing and refusing to pretend it isn’t missing.”"},{"p":"I looked at my crossed-out answer. I had nearly committed to the flight based on the departure airport alone. The fact that I’d caught myself was encouraging. The fact that I’d nearly missed the destination forecast was less encouraging."},{"p":"Mara passed out a second page with more information. The imaginary pilot was a student flying with an instructor. The instructor had conservative personal limits for the day. The destination forecast remained uncertain, and another route offered better conditions for a shorter local lesson."},{"p":"“So what would you do now?” Mara asked."},{"p":"“Stay here and do local maneuvers,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“Maybe,” Mara said. “If the conditions support it.”"},{"p":"“Or use the simulator,” Rafe said."},{"p":"“Also possible.”"},{"p":"I raised my hand. “What if the student is angry because they’ve already missed three lessons?”"},{"p":"Mara leaned against the desk. “Then the student can be angry.”"},{"p":"Rafe snorted."},{"p":"“Feelings matter,” Mara continued. “They just don’t improve the weather. A disappointed student still deserves a safe decision.”"},{"p":"We spent the next hour comparing observations with forecasts. Mara showed us how a forecast could change between updates and why a report from one airport wasn’t a complete description of the route. She also introduced the idea of personal minimums: limits pilots set for themselves based on experience, proficiency, aircraft, and conditions, often more conservative than the legal minimums."},{"p":"“Legal isn’t a synonym for wise,” she said."},{"p":"I wrote that down, though I suspected Uncle Everett would have something to say about how many times he’d learned it the hard way."},{"p":"At the break, I took a picture of the worksheet to send Imani. Before I could, Beckett leaned over my shoulder."},{"p":"“You put no-go before we got the second page?” he asked."},{"p":"“Yeah.”"},{"p":"“Why?”"},{"p":"“The destination forecast.”"},{"p":"He frowned. “I put more information needed. I thought I was being too cautious.”"},{"p":"“I thought I was being too cautious.”"},{"p":"“That’s annoying,” he said."},{"p":"“What is?”"},{"p":"“That we can both be cautious and still disagree.”"},{"p":"“Maybe that’s why Mara gets paid.”"},{"p":"“Not enough,” Mara called from across the room."},{"p":"Rafe came back from the vending machine with a packet of crackers that had split down one side. He held it upright like a leaking bag of sand."},{"p":"“Emergency,” he said. “My snack has experienced structural failure.”"},{"p":"“Declare an alternate,” Beckett told him."},{"p":"Rafe looked at the machine. “The alternate is also broken.”"},{"p":"We laughed, and for a few minutes the empty chair by the window felt less noticeable."},{"p":"Then my phone lit up with a message from Imani."},{"p":"*How’d it go?*"},{"p":"I sent the worksheet photos, making sure the page numbers were visible."},{"p":"*We argued with a fake forecast,* I wrote. *Forecast won.*"},{"p":"Her reply came quickly."},{"p":"*Did Mara ask about personal minimums?*"},{"p":"*Yes. You would have liked it.*"},{"p":"Three dots appeared, disappeared, then appeared again."},{"p":"*Grandma’s appointment took longer than expected. We’re still waiting.*"},{"p":"I stared at the message. I wanted to ask what was wrong, but she’d already told me as much as she wanted to tell me."},{"p":"*I saved you the worksheets,* I wrote. *And Rafe destroyed a bag of crackers.*"},{"p":"*So a normal Saturday.*"},{"p":"*Pretty much.*"},{"p":"When class ended, I stayed behind to put the chairs back in rows. Mara rolled up the map. Outside, the sky was hazy toward the west, though the runway was still easy to see."},{"p":"“Can I ask something?” I said."},{"p":"“You just did.”"},{"p":"“Why do we spend so much time talking about flights we don’t take?”"},{"p":"Mara tucked the map into its tube. “Because you already know what you hope to do on a good flying day. The hard part is making decisions when the day isn’t what you hoped for.”"},{"p":"I thought about the worksheet. The fictional student had waited three weeks for a lesson. That detail had bothered me more than the weather numbers, which was probably why Mara had included it."},{"p":"On the drive home, Dad asked what we’d learned."},{"p":"“Mostly that I can’t tell you whether a flight is safe from one nice-looking weather report.”"},{"p":"“Sounds inconvenient.”"},{"p":"“It is.”"},{"p":"He adjusted the air conditioner. “Did you get to fly?”"},{"p":"“No. Classroom day.”"},{"p":"“Disappointed?”"},{"p":"“A little.”"},{"p":"Dad nodded. He didn’t try to make that feeling disappear."},{"p":"I looked through the passenger-side window. A few miles ahead, clouds were gathering over the highway. The traffic moved steadily beneath them, everyone heading somewhere, everyone with a reason to keep going."},{"p":"My phone buzzed again. Imani had sent a picture of a hospital waiting-room vending machine. A handwritten sign on it read OUT OF ORDER."},{"p":"*Rafe’s alternate is closed too,* she wrote."},{"p":"I laughed loud enough that Dad glanced over."},{"p":"“What?”"},{"p":"“Long story.”"},{"p":"“Good one?”"},{"p":"“Not really.” I looked at the photo again. “Parts of it.”"}]},{"t":"Chapter Five — The Weather That Wasn’t There","blocks":[{"p":"On Monday, the sky above the school parking lot looked as if somebody had washed it with a blue sponge and forgotten to put the clouds back."},{"p":"Theo leaned against the bike rack, balancing a basketball on one palm. He was doing it badly. Every few seconds it rolled off and he caught it against his chest."},{"p":"“You coming Friday?” he asked."},{"p":"“Where?”"},{"p":"“My game. You said you’d come.”"},{"p":"“I said I might.”"},{"p":"“You said, ‘Probably, unless the weather is terrible.’” He threw the ball at me. I caught it, mostly because it hit my stomach. “Look up. Terrible weather. Absolutely dreadful.”"},{"p":"“Friday is four days away.”"},{"p":"“That’s why calendars exist.”"},{"p":"I passed the ball back. He missed it. It bounced under a parked car, and we both watched it roll beyond reach."},{"p":"“I hate basketball,” he said."},{"p":"“You play basketball.”"},{"p":"“That’s how I know.”"},{"p":"My phone buzzed. A message from Imani showed a screenshot of an aviation forecast. She’d circled a line of numbers and added a question mark."},{"p":"*Does this mean the clouds will be gone by three?*"},{"p":"I started typing *Yes*."},{"p":"Then I stopped."},{"p":"The screenshot was cropped. I couldn’t see the airport identifier or when the forecast had been issued. It might have been for Hampton Roads. It might have been for somewhere in Oklahoma. There was no way to tell."},{"p":"*Send the whole thing,* I wrote. *And which day?*"},{"p":"She answered a minute later."},{"p":"*Good catch. Saturday. It’s for Richmond, not PVG. Grandma has an appointment there and Dad wanted to know if the drive would be foggy.*"},{"p":"I stared at the message. Imani hadn’t mentioned a Saturday appointment. The last one had been long enough to keep her at the hospital all afternoon."},{"p":"*How is she?*"},{"p":"Three dots appeared. I waited while Theo tried to persuade a passing seventh grader to retrieve his basketball. The seventh grader declined on grounds that the car might explode."},{"p":"*She’s tired,* Imani finally wrote. *They keep changing what they want to check. She says the hospital coffee tastes like wet cardboard.*"},{"p":"*That sounds like her.*"},{"p":"I’d met Mrs. Bellweather twice. The first time she’d told me my haircut looked like I had lost a fight with a hedge. The second time she’d handed me a plate of cookies and told me to eat before Rafe arrived. I liked her immediately."},{"p":"*Want me to ask Mara about the forecast?* I wrote."},{"p":"*No. It’s okay. Dad has a road-weather app. I was practicing.*"},{"p":"I put my phone away. Theo was kneeling beside the car now, reaching with a fallen branch."},{"p":"“Need help?” I asked."},{"p":"“No,” he said, stretching until his ear nearly touched the pavement. “Unless you have a longer arm.”"},{"p":"I got down beside him. Between us, we managed to drag the ball out without scratching the car. When Theo stood, one knee of his jeans had a black smear on it."},{"p":"“You owe me a game Friday,” he said."},{"p":"“I’ll try.”"},{"p":"“That’s a forecast, not a promise.”"},{"p":"“Exactly.”"},{"p":"He groaned so loudly that a teacher looked over."},{"p":"At home, Uncle Everett was sitting at our kitchen table with a small plastic model of an airplane and one of Dad’s chipped mugs. The model had one wing painted dark blue and the other still gray."},{"p":"“Is that mine?” I asked."},{"p":"“No. Yours is the one you abandoned after gluing the landing gear on backward.”"},{"p":"“It was experimental.”"},{"p":"“It certainly was.”"},{"p":"He wore an old navy-blue sweatshirt despite the heat. Mom had opened the back door, and the screen rattled every time a breeze crossed the yard. Everett’s mug smelled like tea. His paintbrush smelled like something that probably shouldn’t be near food."},{"p":"“I had a question,” I said."},{"p":"“That makes two of us. Mine is why your father keeps a screwdriver in the silverware drawer.”"},{"p":"“Because the handle on the dishwasher falls off,” Dad called from the living room."},{"p":"“I have found the answer to mine,” Everett said. “Your turn.”"},{"p":"I sat across from him. “If a forecast says the ceiling is going to improve at three, can you plan to fly at three?”"},{"p":"Everett put down the brush. “You can plan to check again at three.”"},{"p":"“That’s not the same thing.”"},{"p":"“No.”"},{"p":"“But the forecast is made by people who know weather.”"},{"p":"“Yes. And they tell you what they expect, not what they’ve already seen.”"},{"p":"He turned the model by its fuselage, inspecting the paint. I waited for one of his old Navy stories. Instead he said, “Imagine your dad tells you the dishwasher will be fixed by dinner.”"},{"p":"“It won’t be.”"},{"p":"“There you go.”"},{"p":"“That’s not fair to weather forecasters.”"},{"p":"“Probably not. Your dad’s repair record is worse.”"},{"p":"Dad appeared in the doorway. “I can hear you.”"},{"p":"“Good,” Everett said. “Then you can find the screwdriver.”"},{"p":"I laughed, but I was still thinking about the question. “So forecasts are guesses?”"},{"p":"“Informed predictions. Built from observations, models, and people who know how to read them. Useful. Sometimes remarkably good. Still not a promise.”"},{"p":"“Then why bother?”"},{"p":"Everett picked up the model again. “Because deciding with imperfect information is better than deciding with none. You just have to remember which parts you actually know.”"},{"p":"He handed me the brush to paint a narrow stripe along the fuselage. I got about an inch before my hand slipped. Blue paint crossed the gray belly like a crooked river."},{"p":"“Sorry.”"},{"p":"“It’s plastic, Lincoln.”"},{"p":"“It looked better before.”"},{"p":"“Then we’ll fix it after it dries. Don’t smear it while it’s wet.”"},{"p":"I put the brush down. For once, I managed not to touch the mistake again."},{"p":"That evening I sent Imani a message."},{"p":"*Everett says forecast improvement is a reason to check again, not a guarantee.*"},{"p":"She replied with a photograph of a paper cup on a hospital windowsill. The label said COFFEE. The contents looked suspiciously like pond water."},{"p":"*Grandma says this forecast is also inaccurate.*"},{"p":"I laughed, then read the message twice. She was still at the hospital."},{"p":"*Want me to bring the worksheets over tomorrow?*"},{"p":"This time she answered right away."},{"p":"*Maybe. I’ll ask Mom.*"},{"p":"I put my phone away."}]},{"t":"Chapter Six — The Museum on the Map","blocks":[{"p":"Dad announced we were leaving at seven. At seven-oh-two, I discovered my shoes were still wet from the night before."},{"p":"“Why are they wet?” Mom asked."},{"p":"“I washed them.”"},{"p":"“In the sink?”"},{"p":"“With soap.”"},{"p":"“And you thought they’d dry in eight hours?”"},{"p":"“I had a forecast.”"},{"p":"Dad looked at me over the roof of the car. “Your forecast is currently dripping on the driveway.”"},{"p":"I changed shoes. We left at seven twenty-one."},{"p":"We were headed north to the Steven F. Udvar-Hazy Center near Dulles, the enormous aviation museum Uncle Everett had been talking about for months. Mom had found a weekend that worked with her schedule. Dad had packed sandwiches in a cooler that squeaked when you lifted it. Everett was meeting us there, which meant we were also carrying a container of cookies he had left at our house."},{"p":"I’d wanted Imani to come. Mom had even offered to ask her parents, but Imani said she needed to stay close to home that weekend. Her grandmother was back from the hospital, and the family was trying to get into something resembling a routine."},{"p":"Beckett couldn’t make the trip either. Rafe said he could come if we postponed it until a date that was not this Saturday, which was a creative definition of being available."},{"p":"So it was just us."},{"p":"At the first gas stop, Dad asked me to check the route while he wrestled with the pump. A band of rain had crossed northern Virginia overnight, and he wanted to know whether we might drive into it."},{"p":"I opened the weather radar on my phone. A green patch lay west of our route, with a yellow knot near its southern edge."},{"p":"“Looks fine,” I said."},{"p":"Mom, who was sitting in the front passenger seat, turned around. “Fine now, or fine when we get there?”"},{"p":"I checked the radar image’s timestamp. It was twenty minutes old. Then I played the earlier frames to see which way the rain had been moving."},{"p":"“Fine now,” I said. “Might be different in an hour.”"},{"p":"“That’s more useful,” she said."},{"p":"Dad climbed back into the car. “Did we just get a weather briefing?”"},{"p":"“An extremely expensive one,” Mom said. “It cost us a bag of pretzels and two bottles of water.”"},{"p":"The rain reached the highway before we reached the museum. At first it was a mist. Then drops slapped the windshield hard enough that Dad turned the wipers up. The road signs blurred behind spray from the trucks."},{"p":"“It’s supposed to clear,” I said."},{"p":"“I hope so,” Dad answered, and slowed to leave more space behind the car ahead."},{"p":"We drove for ten minutes with the radio turned down. There was nothing dramatic about it. Nobody screamed. Dad didn’t make a speech. We just went slower because we couldn’t see as far, and the trip took longer than planned."},{"p":"I thought about how often I’d been annoyed when Mara canceled a lesson for weather. From the passenger seat, slowing down was easy to accept. I wasn’t the one steering."},{"p":"Inside the museum, the air felt cool enough to make my wet sleeves unpleasant. The building opened around us into a space so large that for a second I couldn’t work out where to look. Aircraft hung overhead. Others sat on the floor with their noses pointed in different directions, like a crowd of enormous machines waiting for someone to call their names."},{"p":"Dad stopped under the wing of a jet and tilted his head back."},{"p":"“Well,” he said. “That’s a lot of airplane.”"},{"p":"Mom pointed to the information desk. “We’re meeting Everett near the entrance, remember?”"},{"p":"“I’m standing near the entrance.”"},{"p":"“You’re walking away from it.”"},{"p":"“I’m exploring near it.”"},{"p":"I saw Everett before they did. He stood by a railing with his hands in his jacket pockets, looking up at an aircraft suspended above the floor. He wasn’t smiling. That was unusual. Usually museums made him look like a kid who’d found the key to a candy store."},{"p":"“Hey,” I said."},{"p":"He turned and gave me a quick one-armed hug. “You made it.”"},{"p":"“We drove through rain.”"},{"p":"“Cars are good for that.”"},{"p":"“Not as good as you think,” Dad said, joining us. “My windshield wipers are filing a complaint.”"},{"p":"Everett laughed. Then he pointed across the hangar toward a sleek black aircraft. “Want to see something built to go very fast?”"},{"p":"We headed toward the SR-71 Blackbird. From a distance it looked less like a regular airplane than a sharp piece of night. Its long body stretched across the floor, black surfaces catching the museum lights in dull highlights."},{"p":"“It looks fake,” I said."},{"p":"“That’s one way to put it,” Everett replied."},{"p":"The display described its reconnaissance work and extraordinary speed. I read the information twice. The numbers were so large that I couldn’t easily imagine them. We had spent hours in a car covering a distance the Blackbird could have crossed in a fraction of the time, under the right conditions."},{"p":"“Could it fly through any weather?” I asked."},{"p":"Everett shook his head. “No airplane gets a free pass from physics. Different aircraft have different capabilities, equipment, and operating procedures. That doesn’t mean every flight is a good idea.”"},{"p":"I looked again at the black fuselage. I had expected the answer to be something exciting about speed and altitude. His answer sounded annoyingly like Mara."},{"p":"A little farther on, we stopped at a display about weather and flight. I lingered over a diagram showing how moisture, temperature, and rising air could affect cloud formation. I had seen similar drawings in class, but standing among actual aircraft made the subject feel less like a worksheet."},{"p":"Dad read over my shoulder. “So the clouds aren’t just floating cotton.”"},{"p":"“No,” I said. “And a cloud doesn’t have to look scary to matter.”"},{"p":"“You can put that on a shirt,” he said."},{"p":"I took a photo of the display to send Imani, then stopped. She’d told me she was tired of people texting her things to cheer her up. Even nice things. Everybody kept asking for updates, and every update made her repeat what she didn’t know."},{"p":"I put my phone back in my pocket."},{"p":"“Everything okay?” Everett asked."},{"p":"“Yeah. I was going to send Imani a picture.”"},{"p":"“Why didn’t you?”"},{"p":"“I don’t know if she wants one.”"},{"p":"He considered that. “You could ask.”"},{"p":"I gave him a look. “That sounds obvious.”"},{"p":"“A lot of useful things do.”"},{"p":"I sent her a message without a photo."},{"p":"*At the museum. Saw something you’d like. Want pictures, or should I save them for later?*"},{"p":"We kept walking. Mom stopped to read an exhibit label. Dad wandered toward a simulator line and returned when he saw how long it was. Everett told me a story about a training flight that had been delayed for weather, then stopped halfway through because he couldn’t remember whether the delay had happened on a Tuesday or a Thursday."},{"p":"“Does that matter?” I asked."},{"p":"“Not really. But if I don’t know, I shouldn’t pretend I do.”"},{"p":"My phone vibrated."},{"p":"*Pictures are good,* Imani wrote. *But only the weird ones. No fifty photos of engines.*"},{"p":"I grinned."},{"p":"*That’s an unreasonable restriction.*"},{"p":"*It’s a personal minimum.*"},{"p":"I took a picture of Dad staring into a souvenir-shop bin of airplane-shaped pencils, his glasses sliding down his nose. I sent that first."},{"p":"Imani answered with six laughing faces."},{"p":"For the first time all day, I felt like she was there with us, just a little. Not because I’d fixed anything. I hadn’t. But she could still laugh at my dad, and that seemed worth sharing."},{"p":"Outside the tall museum windows, the rain had moved east. The clouds were breaking apart in ragged pieces, showing strips of blue behind them."},{"p":"I checked the time, then looked away from my phone and back toward the hangar. We still had hours left, and more airplanes than I could possibly see in one visit."},{"p":"For once, I put the phone away and went to see the airplanes."}]},{"t":"Chapter Seven — The Forecast in My Pocket","blocks":[{"p":"By Monday, the museum had become three different stories in our house."},{"p":"Dad’s version featured a parking garage with an elevator that smelled like wet carpet and a souvenir pencil that cost more than an actual pencil should. Mom’s version involved the exhibits we had managed to see and the ones we hadn’t, with an impressive amount of detail about where we’d parked. My version mostly featured the SR-71 and a text from Imani that said, *Your father looks like he is negotiating with the pencils.*"},{"p":"I was still laughing at it when she called that evening."},{"p":"“I need a favor,” she said, without saying hello."},{"p":"I sat up on my bed. “Sure.”"},{"p":"“Don’t say sure yet.”"},{"p":"“Okay. A cautious maybe.”"},{"p":"“Can you bring my notebook to the academy tomorrow? The blue one. I left it in Mara’s classroom.”"},{"p":"“You’re coming tomorrow?”"},{"p":"There was a pause. On her end, a door closed and somebody called her name from another room."},{"p":"“I think so,” she said. “Grandma’s home. Dad’s taking care of things. I just want to do something normal for an afternoon.”"},{"p":"“I can get the notebook.”"},{"p":"“And don’t ask how she is as soon as you see me.”"},{"p":"“I wasn’t going to.”"},{"p":"“Lincoln.”"},{"p":"“I was absolutely going to.”"},{"p":"She made a noise that might have been a laugh. “I know.”"},{"p":"After we hung up, I found a sticky note and wrote BLUE NOTEBOOK in capital letters. I put it on my phone. Then I went downstairs and asked Dad to drive me to the airport the next afternoon."},{"p":"“What time?” he asked."},{"p":"“Four.”"},{"p":"“Your mother has the car at four.”"},{"p":"“Three-thirty?”"},{"p":"“Still has the car.”"},{"p":"“Five?”"},{"p":"Dad folded his newspaper. “I can take you on the way back from the library, but I need to know before I arrange my afternoon.”"},{"p":"I almost told him Imani needed me. That sounded better than admitting I’d promised a ride I hadn’t arranged."},{"p":"“I should’ve asked first,” I said."},{"p":"“That would have been helpful, yes.”"},{"p":"He picked up his phone to check his schedule. I peeled the sticky note off mine and added ASK DAD BEFORE PROMISING THINGS."},{"p":"The last word ran off the edge."},{"p":"Mara had left the classroom windows open a crack. The blinds clicked against the frames whenever a gust passed. On the whiteboard she’d drawn two columns, one headed WHAT WE KNOW and the other WHAT WE THINK WILL HAPPEN."},{"p":"Beckett was standing on a chair, trying to hang a string of paper clouds from a ceiling tile."},{"p":"“Is that allowed?” I asked."},{"p":"“The chair?” he said."},{"p":"“The whole situation.”"},{"p":"“Rafe is supervising.”"},{"p":"Rafe, sitting at the desk beneath him, held a half-eaten granola bar. “I have never supervised anything in my life.”"},{"p":"“Get down,” Mara said from the doorway."},{"p":"Beckett stepped off the chair. One of the paper clouds caught on his glasses and hung there until Imani reached over and pulled it free."},{"p":"She’d arrived without announcing herself. Her braids were tied back, and there were dark half-moons under her eyes. I handed her the blue notebook."},{"p":"“Thanks,” she said, opening it to check something."},{"p":"“I didn’t read it.”"},{"p":"“You absolutely wanted to.”"},{"p":"“Only the part where you rank us.”"},{"p":"“You’re fourth.”"},{"p":"“There are four of us.”"},{"p":"“Then you know where you stand.”"},{"p":"It felt good to hear her tease me again. I kept that to myself."},{"p":"Mara handed us each a printed weather packet. It had an airport observation, a forecast, and a simple map. Mara explained that the route was fictional, but the reports followed the formats pilots actually use."},{"p":"“You’re planning a daytime training flight,” she said. “For this exercise, pretend your instructor has asked you to recommend whether to launch. You’re not making a real dispatch decision. Tell me what you’d check, and what would make you stop.”"},{"p":"I scanned the first report. Visibility looked fine. The ceiling was comfortably above the ground. Winds were light. The forecast for the destination, though, mentioned lower clouds later in the afternoon."},{"p":"“Go early,” I said."},{"p":"Mara didn’t write anything. “Why?”"},{"p":"“Because the weather gets worse later.”"},{"p":"“How much earlier?”"},{"p":"I looked at the route distance. I hadn’t worked out the flight time. I hadn’t checked whether the forecast period covered our arrival. I’d seen one good observation and one bad possibility and decided I could squeeze between them."},{"p":"“I don’t know yet,” I admitted."},{"p":"Rafe leaned over his paper. “The forecast doesn’t tell us exactly when the ceiling will drop.”"},{"p":"“It gives a time window,” I said."},{"p":"“Right. A window. Not an appointment.”"},{"p":"Imani tapped the map with her pencil. “And there’s no information here about what happens if we have to turn back after the weather changes.”"},{"p":"“That’s something you’d want to investigate,” Mara said. “What else?”"},{"p":"Beckett pointed to the observation time. “This was issued almost an hour ago.”"},{"p":"“Good catch.”"},{"p":"I looked down at the number I’d skipped. He was right."},{"p":"Mara wrote AGE OF INFORMATION beneath WHAT WE KNOW, then drew an arrow toward the second column."},{"p":"“A METAR is a report of observed conditions at a particular place and time,” she said. “A TAF is a forecast for an airport area over a period of time. Neither is a promise about what you’ll see from the cockpit.”"},{"p":"“So there’s no answer?” I asked."},{"p":"“There are decisions. Those aren’t the same thing.”"},{"p":"We worked through the packet. I crossed out my first recommendation and wrote NEED MORE INFORMATION. It looked annoyingly vague, so I listed the information: updated observations, forecast timing, conditions along the route, a suitable alternative, and whether the flight could be delayed without causing problems."},{"p":"Mara looked at my page. “That’s a better beginning.”"},{"p":"“Not an answer.”"},{"p":"“A beginning,” she repeated."},{"p":"I was going to argue, but Imani slid her notebook between us. She’d drawn a little square around the word *uncertainty* and added a tiny storm cloud with angry eyebrows."},{"p":"“This is you,” she whispered."},{"p":"“I don’t have eyebrows like that.”"},{"p":"“You do when you’re thinking.”"},{"p":"After class, I carried the paper clouds to the storage cabinet while Beckett collected the tape. Imani stayed at her desk, staring at her phone."},{"p":"“Want us to wait?” I asked."},{"p":"She looked up. “No. My dad’s picking me up.”"},{"p":"“Okay.”"},{"p":"I made it three steps before she called my name."},{"p":"“Grandma asked whether you still fly those little paper things,” she said."},{"p":"“The gliders?”"},{"p":"“Yeah. She remembered the one you sent me last year. The one with the crooked wing.”"},{"p":"“That was a prototype.”"},{"p":"“It crashed into our kitchen lamp.”"},{"p":"“An ambitious prototype.”"},{"p":"Imani smiled, but she didn’t look down at her phone again."},{"p":"“Tell her I’ll make a better one,” I said."},{"p":"“Don’t promise that. You know how your planes are.”"},{"p":"“A less crooked one, then.”"},{"p":"“Maybe.”"},{"p":"Outside, the late-afternoon sky had that bright, washed-out look it got after a humid day. I could see clouds building far away, but I couldn’t tell whether they’d bring rain here. For once, not knowing didn’t feel like something I needed to hide."}]},{"t":"Chapter Eight — The Line Nobody Crossed","blocks":[{"p":"The next Saturday, a low layer of clouds sat over Chesapeake like a lid someone had forgotten to lift."},{"p":"At breakfast I checked the aviation weather reports on my phone. I checked them again while brushing my teeth. That was a mistake. Toothpaste slid down my wrist."},{"p":"“Weather won’t improve because you stare at it,” Mom said from the hall."},{"p":"“I’m looking for updates.”"},{"p":"“Then maybe update your sink while you’re at it.”"},{"p":"At the airport, the parking lot was damp, and the windsock barely moved. The quiet bothered me. Wind I understood, at least a little. Wind moved things. It showed itself in flags and trees and the way a ball bent toward the foul line. This weather looked like nothing was happening."},{"p":"Mara met us near the classroom with a clipboard. “We may not fly today,” she said before anyone asked."},{"p":"Beckett groaned. Rafe tipped his head back against the wall. Imani just took off her jacket and folded it over a chair."},{"p":"“We’re still checking conditions,” Mara continued. “I want you to observe the process, not guess the result.”"},{"p":"We gathered around the computer while she reviewed the current airport observation, nearby reports, forecasts, and weather imagery. She explained that an airport could be reporting one set of conditions while another location a short distance away reported something different. She also checked the aircraft’s status and the flight’s planned training objectives."},{"p":"“The ceiling is lifting,” I said when a newer observation appeared."},{"p":"“At this airport, at the time of that report,” Mara said."},{"p":"“Right.”"},{"p":"“What would you want to know next?”"},{"p":"I looked at the map. “Whether it stays that way. And what it’s doing where we’d be flying.”"},{"p":"“Good.”"},{"p":"For a minute I thought we’d actually go. Mara stepped outside to look at the sky, then came back in and made a phone call. I watched her write down a time, underline something, and cross it out."},{"p":"When she finished, she set the clipboard on the desk."},{"p":"“No flight today.”"},{"p":"“But it’s getting better,” I said."},{"p":"“Some observations are improving. The broader picture still isn’t suitable for the lesson we planned, and we don’t need to push it.”"},{"p":"“We could wait.”"},{"p":"“We could. But we have a limited training window, and waiting doesn’t guarantee we’ll get conditions appropriate for the exercise.”"},{"p":"I looked toward the window. The cloud layer had a brighter patch in it. It seemed unfair that something so thin-looking could keep an airplane on the ground."},{"p":"Rafe nudged my elbow. “Your face is doing the thing.”"},{"p":"“What thing?”"},{"p":"“The thing where you argue with weather using your forehead.”"},{"p":"“My forehead has never argued with anything.”"},{"p":"“It is currently filing an appeal.”"},{"p":"Mara heard him. “Appeal denied.”"},{"p":"Even I laughed at that, although I didn’t want to."},{"p":"Instead of flying, we moved into the classroom for a weather-decision game. Mara spread cards across the table. Each card held one new piece of information: a change in visibility, a revised forecast, a delayed departure, a passenger who felt unwell, a runway closure, or a fuel stop that might not be available."},{"p":"“You’re not trying to collect enough good cards to cancel out the bad ones,” she told us. “You’re trying to make a sensible plan with the information you have.”"},{"p":"Beckett immediately invented a scoring system."},{"p":"“There are no points,” Mara said."},{"p":"“There could be.”"},{"p":"“There aren’t.”"},{"p":"“A missed opportunity.”"},{"p":"My team was Imani and Rafe. We started with a pretend flight to a nearby airport in good weather. Then Mara handed us a card saying the destination forecast had worsened. Rafe suggested waiting. Imani asked how long. I said we should pick a different destination."},{"p":"“Why that one?” she asked."},{"p":"“Because the forecast is better.”"},{"p":"“And?”"},{"p":"I checked the chart. The new airport was farther away. We’d need to reconsider the route, fuel planning, and whether it still made sense for the lesson."},{"p":"“And I picked it too fast,” I said."},{"p":"Rafe held up another card. “Also, our imaginary passenger is airsick.”"},{"p":"“We have a passenger?”"},{"p":"“Apparently. He has terrible luck.”"},{"p":"“The passenger is a cardboard square,” Imani said."},{"p":"“That doesn’t mean he feels good.”"},{"p":"We eventually chose to postpone. Beckett’s team did too, but only after a long debate over whether they could complete a shorter lesson closer to the airport. Mara asked both groups to explain what would have to change before they’d reconsider."},{"p":"I expected her to announce a winner. Instead she asked us to swap plans and find weak spots in the other team’s reasoning."},{"p":"“That’s mean,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“That’s review,” Mara answered."},{"p":"He studied our page for twenty seconds. “You forgot to check whether the alternate airport has the services you need.”"},{"p":"I leaned over his team’s plan. “You never said what you’d do if the clouds lowered while you were waiting.”"},{"p":"He frowned. Then he drew a box and wrote DEPARTURE CUT-OFF TIME."},{"p":"“That’s fair,” he said."},{"p":"It was the nicest thing he’d said to me all morning."},{"p":"At lunch we sat on the low wall outside the classroom with sandwiches from the vending area. The clouds had begun breaking up, and a stripe of blue appeared above the far hangars."},{"p":"“Now it’s perfect,” I said."},{"p":"“Now it’s lunchtime,” Rafe said through a mouthful of bread."},{"p":"“That’s not an aviation category.”"},{"p":"“It should be.”"},{"p":"Imani had a folded paper glider sticking out of her notebook. I recognized the shape. I’d made it the night before, then remade it after the first one veered into the refrigerator. This one had a small blue stripe on one wing."},{"p":"“You actually brought it,” I said."},{"p":"“Grandma wanted to see whether you improved.”"},{"p":"“And?”"},{"p":"“She said the fold on the left side is still crooked.”"},{"p":"“That’s brutal.”"},{"p":"“She also said she likes it.”"},{"p":"Imani smoothed the paper carefully, as if she were flattening a photograph."},{"p":"I wanted to ask about her grandmother. The question was right there, ready to jump out. Instead I tore my sandwich wrapper into a narrow strip and watched it flutter in the light breeze."},{"p":"“Do you want to talk about her?” I asked."},{"p":"Imani thought for a while. “Not right now.”"},{"p":"“Okay.”"},{"p":"She looked relieved, which made me glad I’d asked it that way."},{"p":"We sat without talking for a minute. Across the field, an airplane taxied slowly toward the runway. I watched it until a building blocked my view. I didn’t know where it was going or what conditions its pilot had checked. I knew only that our airplane would stay parked, and that Mara had made the call after considering more than the patch of blue over our heads."},{"p":"I still wished we’d flown."},{"p":"That part hadn’t changed."},{"p":"But when Dad arrived, I didn’t tell him the weather had ruined the day. I told him we hadn’t flown, and that Beckett had tried to turn a weather briefing into a competitive sport."},{"p":"“Did he win?” Dad asked."},{"p":"“There weren’t points.”"},{"p":"“That wasn’t my question.”"},{"p":"“He thinks he did.”"},{"p":"Dad started the car. A thin beam of sunlight came through the windshield and landed on the folded weather packet in my lap. In the margin, beside my first recommendation, I’d written a note for next time: *What information am I missing?*"},{"p":"I tucked the paper into my bag before it could slide under the seat."}]},{"t":"Chapter Nine — The Ceiling in the Hallway","blocks":[{"p":"On Tuesday, the school hallway smelled like floor wax, damp sneakers, and somebody’s breakfast sandwich. The smell could have survived a hurricane."},{"p":"Theo was waiting beside my locker with a cardboard tube under his arm."},{"p":"“Please tell me that’s not a telescope,” I said."},{"p":"“It’s a poster.”"},{"p":"“A poster of what?”"},{"p":"“The sky.”"},{"p":"“That’s worse.”"},{"p":"He pulled the cap off the tube and unrolled a sheet of blue construction paper. Three cotton balls were glued across the top. Under them, in black marker, he’d written: CLOUDS: WATER IN THE AIR."},{"p":"“Science fair,” he said. “I need you to tell me whether this is stupid.”"},{"p":"I studied the cotton balls. One had fallen halfway off and was hanging by a single string of glue."},{"p":"“That’s the most realistic part.”"},{"p":"“Lincoln.”"},{"p":"“It isn’t stupid. But clouds aren’t cotton, and not all clouds are at the same height.”"},{"p":"“I know they’re not cotton. They wouldn’t fit in the sky if they were.”"},{"p":"“You asked.”"},{"p":"He rolled the poster up hard enough to wrinkle it. “I asked whether it was stupid. Not for eleven corrections.”"},{"p":"I shut my locker. The metal door made a noise like a dropped baking sheet."},{"p":"“Sorry.”"},{"p":"“You say that like you’re returning a library book.”"},{"p":"“My dad works at a library. It’s genetic.”"},{"p":"Theo tried not to smile. He didn’t quite succeed."},{"p":"We had science together after lunch. I spent most of the morning thinking about his poster instead of the history quiz I should have been thinking about. When we got to class, I asked whether I could help him redo the cloud section. He said I could cut out labels, provided I didn’t give a speech."},{"p":"“How long is a speech?” I asked."},{"p":"“Longer than a sneeze.”"},{"p":"We worked at the back table with scissors that barely cut and a glue stick so dry it squeaked. Theo wanted three cloud types. I suggested showing low, middle, and high clouds, then stopped myself before trying to squeeze every cloud name I’d learned into a corner of the page."},{"p":"“Here’s the thing,” I said, keeping my voice down. “A ceiling isn’t the highest cloud. It’s the height above the ground of the lowest layer reported as broken or overcast—or the vertical visibility when the sky is obscured.”"},{"p":"Theo looked at the poster. “So if there’s one little cloud, that doesn’t make it a ceiling?”"},{"p":"“Right.”"},{"p":"“You could’ve said that first.”"},{"p":"“Probably.”"},{"p":"He wrote LOWEST BROKEN OR OVERCAST LAYER on a yellow strip. Then he held it up."},{"p":"“Are those actual words teenagers use?”"},{"p":"“Only the popular ones.”"},{"p":"By the end of class, our cloud diagram had more labels than Theo wanted and fewer than I wanted. It looked good from three feet away. From closer up, one of the cotton balls had my thumbprint pressed into it."},{"p":"After school, I found a message from Imani."},{"p":"*Can you come by for half an hour? Grandma wants to show you something.*"},{"p":"I read it twice. The last time I’d asked about her grandmother, Imani hadn’t wanted to talk. Now she’d invited me over, and I couldn’t tell whether the invitation was a good sign or a bad one."},{"p":"*I can ask Dad for a ride,* I wrote. *What is it?*"},{"p":"*If I tell you, it ruins the surprise.*"},{"p":"*Is the surprise that she hates my paper airplanes?*"},{"p":"*That’s not a surprise.*"},{"p":"Dad drove me over after his shift. He parked by the curb, where the grass had grown into a crack in the concrete."},{"p":"“Call when you’re ready,” he said. “And don’t stay if they’re tired.”"},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"“I know you know. That’s why I’m saying it once instead of five times.”"},{"p":"Imani opened the door before I knocked. She had on mismatched socks, one purple and one yellow, and there were flour smudges on her shirt."},{"p":"“You bake now?” I asked."},{"p":"“Apparently not very well. Come in.”"},{"p":"Her grandmother was sitting at the dining table beneath a window full of potted plants. She looked smaller than I’d expected. There was a blanket over her knees, and she had the sort of sharp eyes that made me straighten up without being told."},{"p":"“So,” she said, “you’re the boy who can’t fold a straight wing.”"},{"p":"“That’s me.”"},{"p":"“Sit down.”"},{"p":"On the table lay a paper glider made from stiff white card. It had a narrow nose, broad wings, and a paper clip at the front. Beside it was a ruler and a notebook full of little measurements."},{"p":"“Imani told me you like experiments,” her grandmother said."},{"p":"“Sometimes.”"},{"p":"“Good. I can’t walk all over the yard right now, but I can still tell when something flies crooked.”"},{"p":"Imani folded her arms. “She made me test six versions.”"},{"p":"“Five,” her grandmother said."},{"p":"“The one that hit the lamp counts.”"},{"p":"“That was a landing problem.”"},{"p":"We took turns launching the glider down the hallway. We made sure the space was clear and stayed away from the glass cabinet at the far end. The first throw curved left and landed beneath a chair. The second went straight for a few feet, dipped, and skidded across the floor."},{"p":"“What changed?” her grandmother asked."},{"p":"“Imani threw it harder,” I said."},{"p":"“Did she?”"},{"p":"Imani raised an eyebrow. “You didn’t measure that.”"},{"p":"I looked at the glider, then at the notebook. We’d changed the position of the paper clip, too. Two changes at once. I knew better."},{"p":"“We can’t tell which one mattered,” I admitted."},{"p":"“There you go,” her grandmother said."},{"p":"For the next fifteen minutes we moved only the paper clip, marked each position with pencil, and tried to launch from the same spot. Our throws still weren’t identical. One hit my shoe. Another sailed so neatly down the hallway that Imani gave a small shout before remembering to keep her voice down."},{"p":"Her grandmother smiled at that one."},{"p":"When she got tired, she said so without apology. Imani helped her up, and I gathered the paper scraps into a pile. I could hear the soft shuffle of slippers on the kitchen floor."},{"p":"“She’s doing better today,” Imani said after her grandmother went to rest."},{"p":"“I can see that.”"},{"p":"“That doesn’t mean she’s better. Not all the way.”"},{"p":"I put the ruler down. “I wasn’t going to say it did.”"},{"p":"“People keep saying it. One good day and everybody acts like we’re finished worrying.”"},{"p":"Outside, a delivery truck reversed somewhere down the street. Its warning beep sounded through the screen door."},{"p":"“I don’t know what to say,” I told her."},{"p":"“You don’t have to say anything clever.”"},{"p":"“That’s unfortunate. Clever is my whole thing.”"},{"p":"“It’s really not.”"},{"p":"I picked up the glider. One wingtip had a small bend in it. I straightened it carefully and set it beside the notebook."},{"p":"Imani walked me to the door. Before I stepped outside, she said, “She asked me yesterday whether you were coming back.”"},{"p":"“Tell her I need a rematch.”"},{"p":"“She’ll win.”"},{"p":"“Probably.”"},{"p":"At home I found Theo’s science poster folded in my backpack. The yellow label had come loose. I pressed it back into place and thought about what I’d told him: a ceiling was a specific measurement, not a feeling you got when you looked up."},{"p":"I wished there were a measurement for how much you could worry about someone. There wasn’t. I checked my phone once more before bed."}]},{"t":"Chapter Ten — The Missing Hour","blocks":[{"p":"Saturday’s weather briefing started with a clock."},{"p":"Not a real clock. Mara had drawn one on the whiteboard with a marker that squealed as she made the circle. The hands pointed to ten fifteen."},{"p":"“This is now,” she said. “You’re planning a short local lesson. Here’s a weather report from eight fifteen.”"},{"p":"Rafe lifted his hand. “I have a question.”"},{"p":"“Go ahead.”"},{"p":"“Why does your clock have thirteen numbers?”"},{"p":"Mara turned around. There was a small thirteen squeezed between twelve and one."},{"p":"“Because I was talking while I drew it. Ignore the thirteen.”"},{"p":"“Can we use it as an extra hour?”"},{"p":"“No.”"},{"p":"“Worth asking.”"},{"p":"We sat around the classroom table with printed reports and a chart. Rain had passed through overnight, leaving puddles in the seams of the parking lot. Outside the window, a line of low clouds moved over the hangars."},{"p":"I checked the report. Visibility had been good at eight fifteen, and the sky conditions had looked workable for our fictional flight. I started writing YES in the decision box."},{"p":"Then I stopped."},{"p":"“This report is two hours old,” I said."},{"p":"“Correct,” Mara answered."},{"p":"“What happened since then?”"},{"p":"“You tell me what you’d check.”"},{"p":"Imani already had her pencil moving. “Current observation. Forecast update. Radar, with its time stamp. Maybe airport cameras if they’re available and useful.”"},{"p":"“And we’d look outside,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“Looking outside is useful,” Mara said. “But remember what Lincoln learned last time. One patch of blue doesn’t tell you what’s happening along the whole route.”"},{"p":"I crossed out YES so hard the pencil tore a little hole in the page."},{"p":"Mara passed around a newer report. The visibility was lower, and the ceiling had dropped. The conditions were still above some legal minimums, but the scenario involved a training flight and personal limits we’d agreed on ahead of time. Those were more conservative."},{"p":"“So no,” I said."},{"p":"“Maybe no,” Mara replied. “Explain your decision and what options you have.”"},{"p":"I looked at the blank lines under the question. I could write a perfect answer about waiting or postponing. I could even make it sound as though I’d never wanted to fly in the first place."},{"p":"Instead I wrote: *I would want a newer observation and a better trend before deciding. If conditions stay below our planned minimums, we don’t go.*"},{"p":"Mara read it over my shoulder. “That’s a start.”"},{"p":"I waited for the part where she told me what I’d missed."},{"p":"“What if the weather improves in half an hour?” she asked."},{"p":"“We check again.”"},{"p":"“And if you have to be home by noon?”"},{"p":"I glanced at the clock with thirteen numbers. “Then we might not have time for the lesson.”"},{"p":"“Even if the sky turns beautiful?”"},{"p":"“Even then.”"},{"p":"She nodded and moved on."},{"p":"It bothered me that I hadn’t gotten a gold star for the answer. It bothered me more that I still wanted one."},{"brk":1},{"p":"We were supposed to go outside after the briefing to practice identifying clouds from the ramp, staying with Mara in the designated observation area. Before we could, a sharp crack of thunder came from somewhere beyond the airport. It wasn’t close enough to rattle the windows, but everybody stopped talking."},{"p":"Beckett went to the glass. “That wasn’t in the forecast we read.”"},{"p":"“Weather doesn’t check our worksheets,” Mara said."},{"p":"A second rumble followed, lower and longer. Mara kept us inside and checked the latest weather information. A small thunderstorm had developed west of the airport, and the radar image on her screen showed a growing patch of precipitation. She pointed to the time displayed beside the image."},{"p":"“How old is this picture?”"},{"p":"“A few minutes,” Imani said."},{"p":"“And could the storm have changed since it was taken?”"},{"p":"“Yes,” we answered, almost together."},{"p":"Mara asked us to put our pencils down. The lesson was no longer about filling out the scenario correctly. We watched her compare the information available, check the broader weather picture, and make the straightforward decision to keep our group indoors. No one suggested going outside for a better look."},{"p":"The thunder faded after a while. Rain ticked against the classroom window, then came harder, then eased again. A pilot came in from the adjoining office with wet shoulders and a paper cup of coffee. He and Mara exchanged a few words too quietly for us to hear."},{"p":"“Did you see that?” Rafe whispered."},{"p":"“The rain?” Beckett said. “A little.”"},{"p":"“No. Nobody panicked. They just changed what they were doing.”"},{"p":"I hadn’t noticed that until he said it."},{"p":"At lunch we ate at the table instead of the low wall outside. Beckett had brought pretzels in a plastic container that looked large enough to hold a spare airplane tire. He offered them around, except he kept pulling the container away every time Rafe reached in."},{"p":"“You take too many,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“I take the amount required by my body.”"},{"p":"“Your body is a vacuum cleaner.”"},{"p":"I took one pretzel and checked my phone. Theo had sent a picture of his finished science poster. The cotton balls were still crooked. He’d put a tiny cartoon airplane beneath the cloud labels, with a speech bubble that said, *ASK FOR THE LATEST REPORT.*"},{"p":"I laughed."},{"p":"“What?” Imani asked."},{"p":"I showed her."},{"p":"“Your friend is learning,” she said."},{"p":"“He’s going to be unbearable.”"},{"p":"“He learned from you.”"},{"p":"“That’s what I’m afraid of.”"},{"p":"My phone buzzed again. This time it was Dad: *Roads wet. I’ll pick you up at the main entrance. Don’t make me search the hangars.*"},{"p":"I typed back: *Understood. No hangar hide-and-seek.*"},{"p":"The rain had almost stopped by the time we packed up. Mara gave us one last question to think about before the next session."},{"p":"“When did today’s plan change?”"},{"p":"“When we heard thunder,” Rafe said."},{"p":"“Maybe before that,” I said. “When we saw the newer report, we already knew the original plan wasn’t right anymore.”"},{"p":"Mara tipped her head. “That’s a useful distinction.”"},{"p":"Outside, the pavement shone under a break in the clouds. A narrow strip of sunlight reached across the ramp, bright enough to make the puddles look silver. I stood under the entrance awning with Beckett while we waited for our rides."},{"p":"“Would you have gone if the first report had been current?” he asked."},{"p":"“I don’t know. Depends on the rest of the information.”"},{"p":"He looked at me sideways. “That’s a terrible answer for an argument.”"},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"“Hard to fight with.”"},{"p":"“You could try.”"},{"p":"“I’m saving my energy.”"},{"p":"Dad’s car pulled up with the wipers still running, although there was no rain on the windshield. I opened the door and smelled the library’s old-paper scent mixed with the peppermint gum he kept in the console."},{"p":"“Did you fly?” he asked."},{"p":"“No.”"},{"p":"“Did you learn anything?”"},{"p":"I watched Beckett cross the pavement toward his mother’s car. The clouds beyond the airport were already changing shape. By dinner, somebody might remember the day as mostly sunny."},{"p":"“Yeah,” I said. “A report can be accurate when it’s issued and too old to rely on later.”"},{"p":"Dad pulled away from the curb. “Sounds like the traffic app on my phone.”"},{"p":"“Except with airplanes.”"},{"p":"“Higher stakes. Worse parking.”"},{"p":"I laughed, then opened my notebook and added the date beside the latest weather report. I underlined it once. This time, the paper stayed intact."}]},{"t":"Chapter Eleven — The Part I Left Out","blocks":[{"p":"The trouble started with six muffins in a paper bag."},{"p":"Dad had picked them up from the bakery near the library because he believed buying six muffins was cheaper than buying four. He could explain the math. He usually did it while eating the extra two and saying the word *value*."},{"p":"I brought the bag to the academy Saturday. Imani had been skipping breakfast lately—not always, but often enough that I’d noticed."},{"p":"“Are those for everyone?” Rafe asked as soon as I walked in."},{"p":"“Technically.”"},{"p":"“Excellent. I am everyone.”"},{"p":"He reached for a blueberry muffin. I held the bag higher."},{"p":"“Save one for Imani.”"},{"p":"Rafe froze with his hand in midair. “I was going to.”"},{"p":"“You were going to eat three.”"},{"p":"“I contain multitudes.”"},{"p":"Beckett took a muffin without speaking. He had a little yellow smear on the sleeve of his sweatshirt, and when I asked about it he said, “Paint,” in the tone of someone who had already decided not to answer follow-up questions."},{"p":"Imani arrived eight minutes late. I checked the clock twice. She wore the same purple backpack as always. One strap had been repaired with dark thread. She spotted the muffins and smiled. Her eyes stayed tired."},{"p":"“Your dad’s bakery?” she asked."},{"p":"“He doesn’t own it. He just keeps them in business.”"},{"p":"She chose a plain muffin and tucked it into her backpack. I wanted to tell her she could eat it now. I also knew how irritating it was when people monitored every bite of your food, so I didn’t."},{"p":"Mara had spread three printed weather maps across the tables. On the whiteboard she’d written: *WHAT WOULD CHANGE YOUR MIND?*"},{"p":"“That seems threatening,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“Only if you’re attached to being right,” Mara answered."},{"p":"She gave each of us a fictional Saturday flight plan. The airplane, route, and weather conditions were based on the kind of decisions a pilot might face, but we weren’t going anywhere. We were supposed to compare observations, forecasts, and what we could actually see outside."},{"p":"The departure airport had good visibility. The destination was forecast to improve around midday after a morning of low clouds. There was a broad area of rain farther west."},{"p":"“We could wait for the forecast improvement,” I said."},{"p":"“We could,” Mara replied. “What else?”"},{"p":"“Call the flight off,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“Also possible.”"},{"p":"Rafe pointed at the map. “What about another destination?”"},{"p":"“Maybe. Would that make sense for the purpose of the flight? What would we need to check?”"},{"p":"Imani hadn’t spoken. She sat with her pencil across the page, looking at the clock above the door."},{"p":"“You think the forecast is wrong?” I asked her."},{"p":"“I think it’s a forecast,” she said."},{"p":"“That’s not an answer.”"},{"p":"“It’s the only one I’ve got.”"},{"p":"I was about to argue when her phone buzzed. She glanced at it, then turned it facedown."},{"p":"Mara handed out an updated destination report. The ceiling had improved a little, but not enough for the training scenario’s planned minimums. The visibility was better. The rain was still moving."},{"p":"“Your original departure time is in forty minutes,” Mara said. “What do you do?”"},{"p":"I wrote *delay* in my notebook. Then I crossed it out and wrote *reassess before committing to a departure*. That sounded annoyingly like something an adult would say, but it was what I meant."},{"p":"Imani’s phone buzzed again."},{"p":"She raised her hand. “Can I step out for a minute?”"},{"p":"Mara nodded. Imani slipped into the hallway."},{"p":"Rafe watched the door. “Everything okay?”"},{"p":"“Her grandmother’s been in and out of appointments,” I said. “I think today was another one.”"},{"p":"The words were out before I considered whether they were mine to share."},{"p":"Rafe’s expression changed. “Oh. I didn’t know.”"},{"p":"Beckett looked down at his paper. Mara didn’t say anything, but I could feel her attention shift toward me."},{"p":"Imani came back a moment later. Rafe said, “Hey, I hope your grandmother’s okay.”"},{"p":"She stopped just inside the door."},{"p":"“What?”"},{"p":"“Lincoln said—”"},{"p":"“I know what he said.” She looked at me. “I told you because you asked.”"},{"p":"My face got hot. “I didn’t tell them anything private. Just that she had appointments.”"},{"p":"“That was private.”"},{"p":"Nobody moved. Even Rafe, who could usually find a joke in a power outage, stayed quiet."},{"p":"“Sorry,” I said."},{"p":"Imani took her seat. “Okay.”"},{"p":"It wasn’t okay. I could tell by the way she set her pencil parallel to the edge of her notebook and didn’t look at me again."},{"brk":1},{"p":"We finished the exercise in groups. Imani worked with Beckett and Rafe. I worked by myself because I’d managed to create a group of one without anyone actually telling me to leave."},{"p":"Mara came over while I was studying the map."},{"p":"“What’s your recommendation?” she asked."},{"p":"“Don’t depart yet. The destination hasn’t met the conditions we agreed to use, and we need newer information before making another decision.”"},{"p":"“Good. What would you tell your passengers?”"},{"p":"“That we’re waiting.”"},{"p":"“And if they ask whether they’ll still arrive on time?”"},{"p":"I stared at the printed forecast. “I don’t know.”"},{"p":"“Then tell them you don’t know.”"},{"p":"She moved on to Beckett’s table. I kept looking at the map, but the lines and numbers had stopped making much sense."},{"p":"At lunch I found Imani near the side entrance, sitting on the low wall where we’d eaten a dozen times before. Her muffin was beside her, still in its paper wrapper."},{"p":"“Can I sit?” I asked."},{"p":"“It’s a wall.”"},{"p":"I sat a careful distance away. A light breeze pushed a dead leaf across the pavement. It caught under my shoe."},{"p":"“I shouldn’t have said anything,” I told her."},{"p":"“No.”"},{"p":"“I wasn’t trying to make it a big deal.”"},{"p":"“I know. That’s part of why I’m mad.”"},{"p":"I picked the leaf off my sneaker. It broke along the middle vein."},{"p":"“Rafe cares about you,” I said, and regretted it instantly. “Not that that makes it okay.”"},{"p":"“He does. I might have told him myself.”"},{"p":"A baggage cart rattled somewhere on the other side of the building. We listened to it pass."},{"p":"“Everyone keeps asking for updates,” she said. “At home. At school. My aunt calls my mom every night, and then my mom has to explain the same things again. Sometimes Grandma has a good day. Sometimes she doesn’t. I don’t want to turn into the person who gives everybody the hospital report.”"},{"p":"“I get that.”"},{"p":"“Do you?”"},{"p":"I thought about saying yes again. It would have been easier. Instead I shook my head."},{"p":"“Not really. I want to.”"},{"p":"She rubbed her thumb over a crease in the muffin wrapper."},{"p":"“You don’t have to fix it, Lincoln. You don’t even have to say the right thing. Just don’t tell people my stuff.”"},{"p":"“I won’t.”"},{"p":"“And don’t buy me breakfast every time I look tired.”"},{"p":"I looked at the muffin. “Dad bought those.”"},{"p":"“Your dad has a muffin problem.”"},{"p":"“It’s serious. We’re considering an intervention.”"},{"p":"She gave a small laugh. It faded quickly, but I was glad to hear it."},{"p":"“I do like the plain ones,” she admitted."},{"p":"“I’ll tell him. He’ll pretend it was his idea.”"},{"p":"We sat until Mara called us back inside. Imani didn’t suddenly start talking to me the way she usually did. She did, however, pick up the muffin and take it with her."},{"p":"On the ride home I told Dad I’d made a mess of something with a friend. He didn’t demand the whole story."},{"p":"“Can you repair it?” he asked."},{"p":"“Maybe.”"},{"p":"“Will it happen today?”"},{"p":"“Probably not.”"},{"p":"He nodded and turned onto the main road. “That’s inconvenient.”"},{"p":"“Extremely.”"},{"p":"At a red light, he handed me the bakery receipt from the cup holder. He’d bought six muffins again."},{"p":"“You forgot to throw this out,” I said."},{"p":"“No. I wanted you to see the savings.”"},{"p":"“You spent more money.”"},{"p":"“On a per-muffin basis, I’m a financial genius.”"},{"p":"I folded the receipt into a paper airplane that flew approximately four inches before hitting the glove compartment. Dad said the design needed work. For once, I didn’t argue."}]},{"t":"Chapter Twelve — The Place We Could Turn Around","blocks":[{"p":"On the morning of my next flight, Mara took away the schedule."},{"p":"Not literally. It stayed clipped to the board beside the classroom door. But when I pointed to the time beside my name, she covered it with her hand."},{"p":"“What does this tell you?” she asked."},{"p":"“When I’m supposed to fly.”"},{"p":"“And what doesn’t it tell you?”"},{"p":"“Whether we should.”"},{"p":"“Better.”"},{"p":"I had arrived twenty minutes early with my headset, notebook, and a sandwich Mom had packed in a container that smelled faintly of dishwasher soap. The sky over Chesapeake was pale blue, with higher clouds moving in from the west. It looked like flying weather. I tried not to mistake that for a decision."},{"p":"Mara brought up the latest aviation weather information on the classroom computer. The local observations were favorable for the lesson we’d planned. The forecast, however, showed a possibility of showers later in the afternoon. Farther west, the weather was less settled."},{"p":"“What are we doing today?” she asked."},{"p":"“Local practice. Straight-and-level, turns, and maybe a little ground-track work.”"},{"p":"“Where?”"},{"p":"I pointed to the practice area she’d marked on our chart, away from the airport’s immediate traffic pattern."},{"p":"“What are we not doing?”"},{"p":"“Going west to see if the rain’s really there.”"},{"p":"“An excellent thing not to do.”"},{"p":"We reviewed the current conditions, the forecast, the route to the practice area, and our options if anything changed. Mara explained that she was responsible for the flight and would make the final go/no-go decisions. My job was to participate, ask questions, and speak up if something didn’t seem right."},{"p":"I checked the timestamp on each report without being reminded. That felt good. I didn’t say so."},{"p":"Outside, the Cessna sat on the ramp with its white wings catching the sunlight. I walked through the preflight inspection with Mara supervising. She asked me to identify what I was checking and why. At the fuel sump, she handled the sampling and showed me the clear sample container. We looked for water and other contamination before she disposed of the sample properly."},{"p":"I had done this before. Today, I caught myself checking the time and went back to inspecting the airplane."},{"p":"“Everything looks okay to me,” I said after we’d finished."},{"p":"“That’s an observation,” Mara replied. “Now let’s confirm it against the checklist.”"},{"p":"We did."},{"p":"Inside, the seat belt buckle was hot from the sun. I settled into the left seat and adjusted the headset until the ear cups stopped pinching. Mara took the right seat. We completed the cockpit checks, reviewed the available airport weather and traffic information, and listened on the published common traffic advisory frequency. Hampton Roads Executive had no control tower to give us a taxi clearance. Mara and I visually checked for other aircraft and made appropriate traffic announcements as we taxied. I repeated the runway designation under my breath so I wouldn’t mix up the numbers."},{"p":"“Nervous?” Mara asked."},{"p":"“A normal amount.”"},{"p":"“How much is normal?”"},{"p":"“Enough to remember every embarrassing thing I’ve ever done in an airplane.”"},{"p":"“That sounds like a lot of remembering.”"},{"p":"“I have a strong memory.”"},{"p":"She smiled and continued the checklist."},{"p":"The takeoff was hers. The wheels left the pavement, and the airport slid backward beneath us. A row of buildings shrank into neat rectangles. Roads that looked tangled from the ground became lines you could trace with a finger."},{"p":"Once we were established in the practice area at a safe altitude, Mara transferred control to me using the same clear exchange we’d practiced before."},{"p":"“You have the flight controls.”"},{"p":"“I have the flight controls.”"},{"p":"“You have them.”"},{"p":"The yoke felt familiar and strange at the same time. I watched the horizon, checked the instruments, and tried not to chase every tiny movement. Mara had me hold a heading while she pointed out a long road ahead of us."},{"p":"“Where will the airplane go if you keep this heading and the wind pushes us from the side?”"},{"p":"“Off the ground track we want.”"},{"p":"“So?”"},{"p":"“A small heading correction into the wind. Then check what happens.”"},{"p":"I made the correction. Too much at first. The road began sliding toward the other side of the windshield."},{"p":"“Smaller,” Mara said."},{"p":"I eased the airplane back. The road settled closer to the place I wanted it."},{"p":"“That’s better,” she said. “Don’t stare at the road so hard that you forget the rest of the airplane.”"},{"p":"I glanced at the altimeter, then back outside. My altitude had wandered a little. I corrected it gently."},{"p":"For several minutes, the work was almost quiet. Not easy. Quiet. I could hear the engine and the air moving around the cabin. I could feel how a small movement of my hand changed the airplane’s attitude, then how I needed to wait long enough to see the result."},{"p":"“You’re smiling,” Mara said."},{"p":"“Am I?”"},{"p":"“It’s making me suspicious.”"},{"p":"I stopped smiling. That made her laugh."},{"p":"We practiced a gentle turn, then another. Mara reminded me to scan for traffic and keep my eyes outside. I made one turn too steep and had to correct. I corrected the bank and checked my altitude. Mara let me try again."},{"p":"A little later, Mara took the controls. She pointed out a darker patch of sky on the distant horizon. It wasn’t close to us. It wasn’t something we were flying toward. But the change was visible now: a smudge of gray under the higher clouds."},{"p":"“What do you see?” she asked."},{"p":"“Rain, maybe. Or a darker cloud layer.”"},{"p":"“Good. What don’t you know?”"},{"p":"“Exactly where it is, how fast it’s moving, or what it’s doing between there and here.”"},{"p":"“And how would you find out?”"},{"p":"“Updated weather information. And not by flying over to investigate.”"},{"p":"Mara nodded. She checked the information available to us and considered the time remaining. We still had good conditions where we were, but the purpose of the lesson didn’t require us to stay airborne until the last minute."},{"p":"“I think we’ll head back a little early,” she said."},{"p":"I felt the familiar disappointment rise. We had been doing well. I wanted another turn, another few minutes holding the road beneath the nose, another chance to prove I could do it."},{"p":"“Okay,” I said."},{"p":"She glanced at me. “You can be disappointed.”"},{"p":"“I am.”"},{"p":"“You can also ask why.”"},{"p":"“Why now?”"},{"p":"“Because the weather picture is changing, and we have no reason to use up the time we’ve kept in reserve. If the conditions remain good, we’ll have a straightforward return. If they don’t, I want options.”"},{"p":"I looked at the chart on my lap. The airport was not far away. The flight wasn’t in trouble. That was the point, I realized. We weren’t waiting for trouble to make a decision for us."},{"p":"Mara handled the radio calls and flew us back. I identified landmarks and watched the runway come into view. There was no dramatic weather waiting for us at the airport. The landing was ordinary. I unclenched my hands anyway."},{"p":"Back on the ramp, the sky was still mostly blue. I knew exactly how ridiculous it would sound if I told somebody we’d returned early because of a gray patch far away."},{"p":"In the classroom, Mara asked me to write down three things I’d learned."},{"p":"I wrote: *I can hold a ground track better when I don’t rush the correction.*"},{"p":"Then: *A good-looking sky here doesn’t describe the weather everywhere.*"},{"p":"For the third, I stared at the paper long enough that Mara finished putting away her headset."},{"p":"Finally I wrote: *We came back while coming back was still easy.*"},{"p":"She read the line and nodded once."},{"p":"Outside, Dad was waiting in the car. He had the window down and the radio playing quietly. I climbed in with my headset case and the sandwich I’d forgotten to eat."},{"p":"“How’d it go?” he asked."},{"p":"“Really good. We came back early.”"},{"p":"“Those two things go together?”"},{"p":"“Today they did.”"},{"p":"I told him about the road, the wind correction, and the darker clouds. He listened until I started explaining ground track with both hands and nearly knocked his sunglasses off the dashboard."},{"p":"“Use the sandwich as an airplane,” he suggested."},{"p":"“That’s disgusting.”"},{"p":"“It’s aerodynamic if you hold it right.”"},{"p":"At home I checked my phone. There was a message from Imani in the group chat, answering a question Rafe had asked about the weekend assignment. Nothing personal. Just the answer, followed by a drawing of a cloud with an angry face."},{"p":"I typed *How’s your grandmother?* and stared at it."},{"p":"Then I deleted it."},{"p":"Instead I sent a picture of my flight notes with the third line circled. Beckett replied with a thumbs-up. Rafe wrote, *CONGRATULATIONS ON LANDING AT THE PLACE YOU LEFT FROM.*"},{"p":"A minute later, Imani added: *That actually sounds like a pretty good day.*"},{"p":"I set the phone down and ate the sandwich. It was warm, slightly squashed, and tasted like the inside of its plastic container."},{"p":"I didn’t care. I was hungry."}]},{"t":"Chapter Thirteen — The Seat Nobody Took","blocks":[{"p":"On Monday, Imani’s chair was empty."},{"p":"That wasn’t unusual by itself. People missed academy sessions. Rafe had once missed one because his little brother poured orange juice into a game controller and their mother declared a family emergency. Beckett had missed a Saturday when his ride fell through. I’d missed plenty of things because Dad had to work and Mom couldn’t leave the house with the dishwasher making a noise like a bucket of forks."},{"p":"Still, I noticed the empty chair before I noticed the maps on the tables."},{"p":"Rafe noticed me noticing."},{"p":"“Don’t,” he said quietly."},{"p":"“I wasn’t going to.”"},{"p":"“Your face was.”"},{"p":"I sat down. Imani’s chair remained empty. The leg nearest the wall had a strip of blue tape on it because it scraped the floor, and for some reason that bothered me more than the empty seat."},{"p":"Mara set a clear plastic box on the table. Inside were three small cards and a folded paper chart."},{"p":"“Today you’re dispatchers,” she said."},{"p":"“Do we get uniforms?” Rafe asked."},{"p":"“No.”"},{"p":"“A badge?”"},{"p":"“Also no.”"},{"p":"“This profession has terrible benefits.”"},{"p":"“Your salary is zero,” Mara said. “It gets worse.”"},{"p":"She explained that we were a team reviewing a proposed daytime training flight. No airplane would actually leave the ground. The information was real in the ways that mattered: a departure report, a forecast for the destination, a forecast for an alternate airport, and a radar image with a time printed in one corner."},{"p":"The fictional flight would follow visual flight rules, or VFR. The pilot would need weather that met the applicable VFR minimums and conditions suitable for flying by outside visual reference. Mara wrote VFR on the board and underlined it once."},{"p":"“There are regulatory weather minimums,” she said, “and there are personal and school minimums. Those aren’t automatically the same. An inexperienced pilot may choose conditions more conservative than the legal minimums. And no number makes a thunderstorm safe to fly into.”"},{"p":"I copied that down. Beckett drew a tiny storm cloud beside it with a face that looked like our school vice principal."},{"p":"“Here’s the first packet,” Mara said."},{"p":"The departure weather was good. The destination forecast showed improving visibility in the afternoon. The radar image showed scattered rain near the route, but the timestamp was forty-five minutes old."},{"p":"“That’s old,” I said."},{"p":"“Older than you’d like,” Mara replied. “What do you want to know?”"},{"p":"“Whether the rain moved.”"},{"p":"“How would you find out?”"},{"p":"“Get an updated radar picture and current reports along the route.”"},{"p":"“Good.”"},{"p":"Beckett was looking at the forecast. “The destination might still be okay.”"},{"p":"“Might,” I said."},{"p":"“I said might.”"},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"“You said it like I didn’t.”"},{"p":"He turned the paper around and jabbed a finger at the forecast’s valid period. He was right about the time. I was right about the uncertainty. For once we managed not to turn that into a contest."},{"p":"Mara handed us a second card. The destination had not improved as forecast. The cloud ceiling remained low, and the visibility was worse than expected."},{"p":"“Now?” she asked."},{"p":"“Wait,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“Or cancel,” I added."},{"p":"Rafe leaned back. “What if we just choose the other airport?”"},{"p":"“We’d need to check whether it fits the flight’s purpose,” I said. “And the route. And fuel. And weather there.”"},{"p":"“And whether the instructor wants to go there,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“And whether anyone packed lunch,” Rafe added."},{"p":"“Lunch is not a weather minimum,” Mara said."},{"p":"“It is for me.”"},{"p":"We laughed, and for half a second I turned toward the empty chair because Imani would have had something to say about that. Probably something unkind about Rafe’s ability to survive a three-hour drive without snacks."},{"p":"I turned back before anyone caught me."},{"p":"At the break I took my phone outside. The ramp was warm, and the air smelled of cut grass and airplane exhaust. A trainer taxied past, its propeller making the sunlight flicker through the blades. I opened my messages with Imani."},{"p":"The last thing I’d sent was the picture of my notebook from Saturday. She had replied in the group chat, but not to me privately."},{"p":"I typed: *Sorry about what I said. I know you told me in confidence.*"},{"p":"I read it twice. It sounded correct. It also sounded like I’d copied it from a school poster."},{"p":"I deleted it."},{"p":"Then I typed: *I shouldn’t have told them about your grandmother. I did, and you had to deal with it. I’m sorry. You don’t have to answer.*"},{"p":"My thumb hovered over Send. A gust pushed the corner of my paper packet across the bench. I trapped it under my elbow and sent the message."},{"p":"No answer appeared. I hadn’t expected one, which didn’t stop me checking six times."},{"p":"When I went back inside, Mara was stacking the scenario cards."},{"p":"“You okay?” she asked."},{"p":"“Mostly.”"},{"p":"She didn’t ask about Imani. She just held out the last card."},{"p":"“One more question before you go.”"},{"p":"The card showed a fictional flight already in progress. The destination weather had deteriorated. The airplane had enough fuel to reach a suitable alternate with reserve fuel remaining, and the pilot had current information confirming acceptable conditions there."},{"p":"“What would you recommend?” she asked."},{"p":"“Divert before the options get worse.”"},{"p":"“Why not continue and take a look?”"},{"p":"I rubbed the edge of the card with my thumb. “Because seeing whether it’s bad by getting there could leave us with fewer choices.”"},{"p":"Mara nodded. “Good. What information would you still want?”"},{"p":"I started listing it. Updated reports. Conditions along the new route. Terrain and airspace. Fuel calculations. Whether the alternate was actually suitable for the airplane and pilot."},{"p":"She stopped me after the fifth item."},{"p":"“That’s enough for today.”"},{"p":"Outside, Dad was waiting with a library book propped against the steering wheel. He put it down when I climbed in."},{"p":"“How was the weather?”"},{"p":"“On paper? Awful. Outside? Fine.”"},{"p":"“That sounds about right.”"},{"p":"I told him about the exercise while we pulled onto the road. At a red light he asked whether I’d heard from Imani."},{"p":"“No.”"},{"p":"“Did you apologize?”"},{"p":"“Yes.”"},{"p":"“Then give her some room.”"},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"He glanced at me. “You can know a thing and still be bad at doing it.”"},{"p":"“Is that on a library poster?”"},{"p":"“Not yet.”"},{"p":"At home I put my phone on the kitchen counter, faceup. I helped Mom unload groceries. I did my math homework. I took the trash out without being asked, which made Dad suspicious enough to check whether I’d broken something."},{"p":"When I finally looked at the phone, Imani had read my message."},{"p":"She hadn’t answered."},{"p":"I turned the screen over and left it there."}]},{"t":"Chapter Fourteen — A Different Kind of Forecast","blocks":[{"p":"Thursday smelled like wet pavement and hot rubber."},{"p":"The storm had passed before dawn. Puddles filled the academy parking lot, and a stripe of mud crossed the walkway. Beckett arrived carrying a cardboard box with a torn handle. Rafe followed him, holding an umbrella that had folded inside out."},{"p":"“It’s not raining,” I said."},{"p":"“Tell the umbrella,” Rafe replied. “It refuses to believe me.”"},{"p":"Beckett set the box on a table. Inside were four plastic jars, a small battery-powered fan, two rulers, and what looked like a piece of his mother’s kitchen strainer."},{"p":"“Please tell me you’re not making soup,” I said."},{"p":"“It’s a visibility experiment.”"},{"p":"“With soup equipment.”"},{"p":"“You can be helpful or you can be quiet.”"},{"p":"“Those are difficult choices.”"},{"p":"Mara had asked us to bring simple materials for a demonstration about how visibility changes when light scatters through particles or droplets. We weren’t going to manufacture actual fog in the classroom. Beckett had built a model that used a translucent sheet, adjustable light, and different layers of mesh to make distant markings harder to distinguish. Mara warned us before we started: the box couldn’t measure aviation visibility."},{"p":"“A pilot doesn’t replace reported visibility or an in-flight assessment with a homemade box,” she said. “We’re exploring why things that are easy to see in clear air become difficult to identify when the view is obscured.”"},{"p":"Rafe peered into the box. “I can see a ruler.”"},{"p":"Beckett added another layer of mesh."},{"p":"“Now?”"},{"p":"“A ghost ruler.”"},{"p":"“Technical term,” I said."},{"p":"“Write it down,” Rafe told me."},{"p":"Imani arrived while Beckett was adjusting the light. She paused at the doorway, spotted the box, and frowned."},{"p":"“Why does that look like something confiscated from a science fair?”"},{"p":"“Because Lincoln helped,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“I did not.”"},{"p":"“Exactly.”"},{"p":"Imani set her backpack beside the chair with blue tape. She didn’t look at me, but she didn’t move to a different table either. I decided that was not an invitation to speak."},{"p":"Mara divided us into pairs. Rafe and Beckett worked on the model. Imani and I were assigned the weather packet: a METAR reporting reduced visibility and low clouds, plus a TAF that suggested improvement later in the day."},{"p":"“You can switch,” Mara offered quietly."},{"p":"Imani picked up a pencil. “It’s fine.”"},{"p":"That was not the same as everything being fine. I knew that much."},{"p":"We worked in silence for a few minutes. The METAR used coded groups I’d once thought were impossible to read. Now I could pick out the observation time, visibility, cloud layers, wind, and temperature, though I still had to check the reference sheet for some abbreviations."},{"p":"“The forecast improves after this time,” I said, pointing."},{"p":"“Maybe,” Imani said."},{"p":"“Right. Forecast, not promise.”"},{"p":"She circled the observation time on the METAR. “And this one is already almost an hour old.”"},{"p":"“We’d need a newer report before deciding anything.”"},{"p":"She wrote that down."},{"p":"Across the table, Rafe announced that the ruler had disappeared entirely."},{"p":"“Excellent,” Mara said. “Now imagine the thing you can’t see is a landmark you were relying on to know where you are.”"},{"p":"“I would rely on a different landmark,” Rafe said."},{"p":"“And if the weather has hidden that one too?”"},{"p":"He lowered the model. “I see where this is going.”"},{"p":"“That’s the point,” Mara said. “You want to recognize conditions that may become unsuitable before you’re trapped trying to fix them.”"},{"p":"She drew two columns on the board: *What we know* and *What we assume.*"},{"p":"We filled them in from the weather packet. The destination’s latest observation belonged in the first column. The idea that the clouds would lift exactly when predicted belonged in the second. So did our hope that the weather between the two airports would match conditions at either end."},{"p":"I found myself looking at the columns longer than necessary."},{"p":"“You’re staring,” Imani said."},{"p":"“I’m thinking.”"},{"p":"“That’s new.”"},{"p":"It wasn’t much of a joke, but it was hers. I smiled before I could stop myself."},{"p":"“I deserved that.”"},{"p":"She put her pencil down. “You deserved more than that.”"},{"p":"“Yeah.”"},{"p":"The others were busy arguing over whether the mesh had been stretched evenly. Mara was across the room gathering spare handouts. Imani kept her voice low."},{"p":"“I wasn’t hiding that my grandmother was sick because I was embarrassed,” she said. “I just didn’t want everybody asking me about it all the time. At school. Here. Everywhere.”"},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"“You didn’t know when you told them.”"},{"p":"“No. I didn’t.”"},{"p":"She traced the edge of the forecast with one fingernail. “Rafe was trying to be nice. Then I had to explain why I was upset with him. And he thought he’d done something wrong.”"},{"p":"“I didn’t think about that part.”"},{"p":"“I noticed.”"},{"p":"The words stung. I kept my hands flat on the table instead of folding them into fists under it."},{"p":"“I sent you a message,” I said, then immediately wished I hadn’t. It sounded like I wanted credit for the message."},{"p":"“I read it.”"},{"p":"“Okay.”"},{"p":"She looked toward the window. A truck went past on the road beyond the fence, tires hissing through a puddle."},{"p":"“She’s home,” Imani said. “Grandma. For now. She’s tired and annoyed that everybody keeps telling her to rest.”"},{"p":"“That sounds like her.”"},{"p":"“She made my uncle move a chair because she said it was blocking the good light. He moved it six inches. She said it was perfect.”"},{"p":"I laughed, and Imani did too, just a little."},{"p":"“I’m glad she’s home,” I said."},{"p":"“Me too.”"},{"p":"I wanted to ask whether Imani was still angry. Whether we were friends again. Instead, I kept my mouth shut and walked beside her."},{"p":"Mara called us back to the front. She had another scenario, this time about a pilot who launched because the weather was forecast to improve but discovered the conditions were not improving along the route."},{"p":"“What are the pilot’s options?” she asked."},{"p":"Beckett raised his hand. “Turn back if conditions behind are suitable.”"},{"p":"“Good.”"},{"p":"“Divert to another suitable airport,” Rafe said."},{"p":"“Also good.”"},{"p":"Imani looked at me. “Or don’t launch in the first place when the plan depends on a forecast doing exactly what you hope.”"},{"p":"“That would be my choice,” I said."},{"p":"Mara asked us to explain why. This time I didn’t rush to be first. Imani went through the report times and the missing information. I added that a pilot needed a realistic way to change plans, not just an alternate name written on a sheet of paper."},{"p":"“A backup plan isn’t useful if you haven’t checked whether it works,” Mara said."},{"p":"After class, Rafe tried to put the umbrella back in its sleeve. The bent metal ribs caught on the fabric, and the whole thing sprang open against his chest."},{"p":"“I have been attacked,” he said."},{"p":"Beckett took the umbrella and examined it with the solemn expression he usually reserved for airplane engines."},{"p":"“This is not airworthy.”"},{"p":"“It’s not an aircraft.”"},{"p":"“Not with that attitude.”"},{"p":"Imani laughed properly then, head tipped back, one hand over her mouth. I hadn’t heard that laugh in weeks."},{"p":"Outside, the sun had come out, though a bank of clouds still sat over the western horizon. Imani walked beside me as far as the parking lot."},{"p":"“I might not answer messages much this week,” she said."},{"p":"“Okay.”"},{"p":"“You don’t have to keep checking.”"},{"p":"I glanced at her. “Was it that obvious?”"},{"p":"“You look at your phone like it’s holding your exam results hostage.”"},{"p":"“Fair.”"},{"p":"She shifted her backpack higher. “You can send me airplane pictures. Just not fifty.”"},{"p":"“Forty-nine?”"},{"p":"“Three.”"},{"p":"“Cruel.”"},{"p":"She walked toward her mother’s car. I stood by Dad’s until she got in, then opened my own door."},{"p":"“Everything all right?” Dad asked."},{"p":"“Not everything,” I said."},{"p":"He waited."},{"p":"“Some things are better.”"},{"p":"“Good enough for Thursday,” he said."},{"p":"As we drove away, I looked back at the airport. The clouds beyond it were still there. They hadn’t moved much, at least not from where I could see."},{"p":"I didn’t know what tomorrow’s weather would do. I didn’t know whether Imani would answer if I sent her a picture of an airplane."},{"p":"For once, I could leave both questions unanswered until I had more information."}]},{"t":"Chapter Fifteen — The Day the Sky Looked Fine","blocks":[{"p":"On Saturday morning, the sky over our driveway was the kind of blue that made adults say things like *What a gorgeous day* before they’d even had coffee."},{"p":"Dad was standing beside the car with a paper cup balanced on the roof. He’d brought two folding chairs, a bag of library books he needed to return, and a single banana."},{"p":"“Is that your breakfast?” I asked."},{"p":"“It’s a very efficient breakfast.”"},{"p":"“You bought a banana so you could spend more time putting chairs in the trunk?”"},{"p":"“I have several talents.”"},{"p":"I checked the weather on my phone. A sun icon. Seventy-four degrees. A postcard forecast."},{"p":"“Perfect,” I said."},{"p":"Dad lifted his coffee off the roof. “Perfect for what?”"},{"p":"“Flying.”"},{"p":"“That’s a bigger claim than you think.”"},{"p":"Dad had driven me to enough canceled lessons to know a pretty sky wasn’t a flight plan."},{"p":"“I said *looks* perfect,” I told him."},{"p":"“You said perfect. I was there.”"},{"p":"We drove west with the windows cracked. The air smelled of damp leaves and someone’s charcoal grill, though it wasn’t even nine. I watched little strips of mist hanging over a drainage ditch beside the road. The sunlight caught them and turned their edges white."},{"p":"“See?” I said. “Fog in the ditch, sunshine everywhere else.”"},{"p":"“Very observant.”"},{"p":"“You could sound more impressed.”"},{"p":"“I’m driving. I have to save some of my attention for that.”"},{"p":"At the airport, the windsock hung almost straight down. Beckett’s mother’s car was already parked by the training building. Rafe was sitting on the curb with a packet of crackers, and Imani stood nearby, scrolling through something on her phone."},{"p":"She glanced up when I got out. “Morning.”"},{"p":"“Morning.”"},{"p":"We hadn’t returned to the way things were before I told the others about her grandmother. But she had sent me a photograph of a paper airplane the night before. It was stuck in a lampshade. The message underneath said, *Grandma says this is why pilots need checklists.*"},{"p":"I hadn’t tried to turn it into a serious conversation. I’d sent back, *Was the landing survivable?*"},{"p":"Imani had replied with a skull emoji."},{"p":"For us, that counted as progress."},{"p":"Mara met us inside, where the room smelled faintly of dry-erase markers and burnt toast from the little kitchenette. On the whiteboard she’d written three words:"},{"p":"**WHAT’S MISSING?**"},{"p":"“Your good handwriting?” Rafe offered."},{"p":"“A dangerous opening move,” Mara said. “Try again.”"},{"p":"She handed us a weather packet for a pretend afternoon flight. It included an airport observation, a terminal forecast, a regional weather briefing, and a printed radar image. The route was short enough to seem easy. The departure airport reported good visibility and a high ceiling. The destination forecast looked acceptable too."},{"p":"I reached for the radar page."},{"p":"“That looks clean,” I said."},{"p":"“Does it?” Mara asked."},{"p":"“No precipitation along the route.”"},{"p":"“What time is the image from?”"},{"p":"I found the timestamp in the corner. Forty-seven minutes old."},{"p":"“Oh.”"},{"p":"“What does that tell you?”"},{"p":"“It tells me what was happening forty-seven minutes ago.”"},{"p":"“And?”"},{"p":"“Not what’s happening now.”"},{"p":"Rafe leaned over. “Technically, it tells you what the radar was showing forty-seven minutes ago. Not necessarily exactly what was happening at the ground.”"},{"p":"Mara pointed at him. “Important distinction.”"},{"p":"“He’ll be unbearable all day,” Beckett muttered."},{"p":"“Already am,” Rafe said."},{"p":"The packet included another problem. The destination forecast mentioned a possibility of thunderstorms later in the afternoon. The weather wasn’t certain to turn bad, but there was enough uncertainty that we needed to think carefully about the timing of the flight and whether we’d have a suitable alternative."},{"p":"“So we leave early,” I said."},{"p":"Imani looked at me over the top of her paper. “How early?”"},{"p":"“Before the storms.”"},{"p":"“That’s not a time.”"},{"p":"“Before two, maybe.”"},{"p":"“Where did two come from?”"},{"p":"I checked the forecast again. It didn’t say two. I’d picked the number because it sounded reasonable."},{"p":"“From my imagination,” I admitted."},{"p":"“Popular source,” Beckett said. “Not always reliable.”"},{"p":"I took a pencil and wrote **UNKNOWN** beside my departure time. That felt irritatingly honest."},{"p":"Mara moved between the tables while we worked. She wasn’t asking us to dispatch a real airplane. She wanted us to recognize the information we’d need and the places where a plan could fall apart."},{"p":"“A forecast is a tool,” she said, “not permission to ignore what you observe. And an instructor or pilot in command has to evaluate the complete picture, including the aircraft, route, and their own abilities.”"},{"p":"Outside, the sunlight was still pouring through the windows. I kept noticing it. Part of my brain wanted to use it as proof that the packet was making too much fuss."},{"p":"At break, we went out by the fence. A small airplane taxied toward the runway, its propeller turning into a gray circle. I watched it wait while another aircraft landed."},{"p":"“Think Mara will let you fly today?” Beckett asked."},{"p":"“I don’t know.”"},{"p":"“You always know,” he said."},{"p":"“I always *say* I know. Different thing.”"},{"p":"He made a face, like he couldn’t decide whether to tease me or write down the date."},{"p":"Imani stood a few steps away, pulling apart a granola bar wrapper. She’d barely touched her snack."},{"p":"“How’s your grandma?” I asked, quietly enough that the others wouldn’t hear."},{"p":"“She tried to water the plants yesterday. Mom caught her carrying the watering can and made her sit down. Grandma told her she was acting like an airport security officer.”"},{"p":"“I think I’ve met that security officer.”"},{"p":"“She’s okay today.” Imani looked toward the runway. “At least, that’s what she said this morning.”"},{"p":"I waited. She didn’t add anything, so I didn’t ask for more."},{"p":"When we went back inside, Mara had new information waiting on the board. The latest report showed conditions changing near our fictional destination, and the regional outlook had been updated. Our group’s original plan no longer made sense."},{"p":"“You have ten minutes,” Mara said. “What do you recommend now?”"},{"p":"Beckett wanted to find another destination. Rafe thought we should delay and gather fresh reports. Imani asked whether there was any reason we had to make the flight at all."},{"p":"I stared at the little route line we’d drawn in blue pencil. I’d worked hard on it. I’d measured distances and marked checkpoints and even drawn a tiny airplane at the beginning because Rafe said it looked like a worm without one."},{"p":"Erasing it felt like throwing away work."},{"p":"“We don’t go,” I said."},{"p":"Beckett looked up. “You sure?”"},{"p":"“No. That’s sort of the problem. We don’t have enough good information to make this particular plan worth it. We can do it another day.”"},{"p":"Rafe tapped the worm-shaped route. “Our worm is grounded.”"},{"p":"“He’ll survive,” I said."},{"p":"Mara asked us to explain our reasoning, and we did. Mara pointed out two things we’d missed and one question we’d asked well."},{"p":"After class, I found Dad in the waiting area, asleep in one of the folding chairs he’d insisted on bringing. His banana peel was folded neatly inside an empty paper cup."},{"p":"“How was flying?” he asked when I nudged his shoe."},{"p":"“Didn’t fly. We did weather planning.”"},{"p":"“Ah.”"},{"p":"“The sky looked fine the whole time.”"},{"p":"Dad followed me out to the car. “And was it fine where your pretend flight was going?”"},{"p":"“Not necessarily.”"},{"p":"“There you go.”"},{"p":"I was tempted to tell him that sounded suspiciously like a lesson. Instead I climbed into the passenger seat and buckled up."},{"p":"The sun was still bright. A cotton-ball cloud drifted over the parking lot. Its shadow crossed the asphalt."},{"p":"I didn’t hate the blue sky. I just trusted it a little less than I had that morning."}]},{"t":"Chapter Sixteen — A Number With a Name","blocks":[{"p":"On Monday, Theo brought a shoebox weather station to school."},{"p":"That sounds impressive until you know it consisted of a plastic rain gauge, a thermometer with a cracked suction cup, and a wind vane made from a drinking straw and two pieces of cereal-box cardboard."},{"p":"“The arrow’s backwards,” I said."},{"p":"“Hello to you too,” Theo replied."},{"p":"We were setting up for the science fair in the cafeteria. Someone had spilled orange juice near the entrance. Every third step made a sticky noise. Posters about volcanoes, magnets, and mold crowded the tables. A seventh grader had built a cardboard solar system so large it blocked the emergency exit until a teacher made him move Saturn."},{"p":"Theo held up his wind vane. “It’s not backwards.”"},{"p":"“The point is supposed to show where the wind comes from.”"},{"p":"“It does.”"},{"p":"I leaned closer. The tail was larger than the arrowhead. Wind would push it downwind, leaving the point facing into the breeze. Theo had built it correctly."},{"p":"“Oh,” I said."},{"p":"“You’re welcome.”"},{"p":"“I didn’t say thank you.”"},{"p":"“I know. I’m accepting it anyway.”"},{"p":"We set the shoebox on the table. Theo had written **WHICH WAY IS THE WIND GOING?** in letters that slanted progressively downhill. I offered to straighten the sign. He said no."},{"p":"“You know,” he said, “people might like your help more if you waited for them to ask.”"},{"p":"“People might like your sign more if it wasn’t falling into the ocean.”"},{"p":"“See? That’s exactly what I mean.”"},{"p":"He went to find tape. I watched him walk away and felt the familiar unpleasant warmth behind my ears."},{"p":"It wasn’t the first time someone had told me that. Imani had. Beckett had. Dad had, using nicer words. I could spot a pattern on a weather chart. Apparently, my own behavior required more practice."},{"p":"A little girl from the sixth-grade group approached our table. Her backpack was covered in glow-in-the-dark stars."},{"p":"“Does that tell you how fast the wind is?” she asked, pointing at the cardboard vane."},{"p":"“No,” I said. “It tells you direction. An instrument called an anemometer measures wind speed.”"},{"p":"She nodded and pointed at the label. “But if the arrow points north, does the wind go north?”"},{"p":"I opened my mouth to explain it, then stopped. I had to think about that one."},{"p":"“Wind directions are named for where the air comes *from*,” I said. “So a north wind blows from the north toward the south.”"},{"p":"“That’s confusing.”"},{"p":"“A little.”"},{"p":"“Why not name it where it goes?”"},{"p":"“I don’t know who made that rule. But pilots, weather observers, and lots of other people use the same convention, so we can understand one another.”"},{"p":"She studied the vane. “If I make one at home, can I call it the other way?”"},{"p":"“You can call it Captain Noodle if you want. But if you write down the wind direction, you should explain what you mean.”"},{"p":"She grinned. “Captain Noodle is better.”"},{"p":"After she left, Theo returned with tape and a bag of pretzels."},{"p":"“You didn’t correct her about anything else?” he asked."},{"p":"“I did, actually. But she asked.”"},{"p":"“Remarkable.”"},{"p":"We taped the sign where Theo wanted it, crooked letters and all."},{"p":"At the academy the following afternoon, Mara had a different sort of number waiting for us: a visibility reading in a sample weather observation."},{"p":"“What do you think this tells you?” she asked."},{"p":"Rafe read it aloud. “Ten statute miles visibility.”"},{"p":"“And what doesn’t it tell you?”"},{"p":"“Whether there are clouds above that?” Beckett offered."},{"p":"“Correct. Visibility and ceiling describe different things.”"},{"p":"Imani tapped the sample report. “And it doesn’t mean every place along a route has ten miles of visibility.”"},{"p":"“Exactly.”"},{"p":"Mara put two photographs on the board. In one, a runway was clear beneath a high, gray cloud deck. In the other, low mist made the far end of a paved strip difficult to see, even though patches of blue were visible overhead."},{"p":"“Which one is safer?” she asked."},{"p":"Nobody answered immediately."},{"p":"That was new for us."},{"p":"“Not enough information,” I said at last. “We’d need actual observations, the kind of flight, the aircraft, and what the pilot is qualified and comfortable doing.”"},{"p":"“Good start,” Mara said. “And photographs can be misleading. They may be old, taken from different locations, or framed to hide what matters.”"},{"p":"Rafe raised his hand. “Could we take a picture of Beckett’s lunch and make it look edible?”"},{"p":"“No technology exists,” I said."},{"p":"Beckett pulled a sandwich from his bag. It was flattened so thoroughly that the bread had become one layer."},{"p":"“It’s peanut butter,” he said. “It doesn’t have to look like a wedding cake.”"},{"p":"Mara gave us a practice assignment. We had to compare a set of observations taken at different times and identify which changes mattered for a hypothetical local training flight. Mara reminded us that we were studying trends, not authorizing a flight or setting our own legal minimums."},{"p":"Imani and I ended up at the same table. Beckett and Rafe sat across from us, arguing about whether a high ceiling mattered if visibility was getting worse."},{"p":"I reached for the pencil just as Imani did. Our fingers bumped."},{"p":"“Sorry,” I said."},{"p":"“You can write,” she replied. “Your numbers are easier to read.”"},{"p":"I wrote the observation times down the left side of the page. Reported visibility fell with each observation. The lowest reported cloud layer was getting lower, too."},{"p":"“If this keeps happening,” I said, “the conditions could become worse than the ones we’re planning around.”"},{"p":"“Could,” Imani repeated. “We don’t know that it will.”"},{"p":"“Right. Could.”"},{"p":"She looked at the page for a while. “Grandma has another appointment Friday.”"},{"p":"I kept my pencil still. “Do you want me to know how it goes?”"},{"p":"“Maybe. I don’t know yet.”"},{"p":"“Okay.”"},{"p":"“That’s all?”"},{"p":"“I could give you a twenty-minute speech about how doctors work, but I don’t think you’d enjoy it.”"},{"p":"“I would actually pay you not to.”"},{"p":"“How much?”"},{"p":"“Two dollars.”"},{"p":"“That’s insulting.”"},{"p":"“Three, if you stay quiet through Friday.”"},{"p":"I pretended to consider it. “I have expenses.”"},{"p":"She laughed, then went back to the weather sheet."},{"p":"At the end of class, Mara gave us a new scenario for the next session. A planned training flight would depart under good conditions, but a changing forecast raised questions about whether the airport would remain suitable for the return."},{"p":"“Don’t solve it tonight,” she said. “Read the packet. Write down the questions you’d need answered. We’ll work through it together.”"},{"p":"Rafe held up his hand. “Can one of my questions be why you enjoy making us nervous?”"},{"p":"“It can,” Mara said. “I won’t promise to answer it.”"},{"p":"On the drive home, Dad asked whether I had homework."},{"p":"“Yes.”"},{"p":"“Math?”"},{"p":"“Weather.”"},{"p":"“What kind of weather?”"},{"p":"“The kind where you’re supposed to figure out what you don’t know.”"},{"p":"He nodded as if that made complete sense. Maybe it did to a librarian. They spent a lot of time looking for things people didn’t know."},{"p":"I opened the packet on my lap, but I didn’t start filling in answers. I made a list of questions instead. When was the forecast issued? How quickly had the conditions changed? What would we do if the weather got worse while we were away?"},{"p":"Then I added one more, near the bottom."},{"p":"**Who gets to decide when enough is enough?**"},{"p":"I wasn’t sure whether Mara would like that question. I wasn’t even sure what I meant by it yet."},{"p":"I left it there anyway."}]},{"t":"Chapter Seventeen — The Question at the Bottom","blocks":[{"p":"The next morning, I found Dad trying to rescue toast with a butter knife."},{"p":"“Please don’t do that,” I said."},{"p":"He looked at the knife, then at me. “It’s unplugged.”"},{"p":"“That’s what people say right before somebody has to explain a very embarrassing accident.”"},{"p":"“The toast is stuck.”"},{"p":"“I can see the toast.”"},{"p":"“Then we’re both making progress.”"},{"p":"He eased the cold, mangled slice free and dropped it on a plate. Mom walked past, took one look, and poured cereal into a bowl."},{"p":"I sat at the table with Mara’s packet. I’d written six questions on the first page and another four on the back. The last one still bothered me: Who gets to decide when enough is enough?"},{"p":"Dad saw the page while reaching for his coffee."},{"p":"“That a philosophy assignment?”"},{"p":"“Flight school.”"},{"p":"“That sounds worse.”"},{"p":"I explained the scenario. An instructor and student planned a daytime local training flight. The departure airport looked fine. The forecast suggested lower clouds might arrive before they got back. Other airports were nearby, but the packet didn’t say whether their weather or facilities made them suitable alternatives. It also didn’t tell us how much fuel the airplane had, how experienced the instructor was, or whether the lesson could be shortened."},{"p":"Dad listened while wiping toast crumbs from his glasses. “Why not just cancel?”"},{"p":"“Because we don’t know if they need to.”"},{"p":"“Then why not just go?”"},{"p":"“Because we don’t know if they should.”"},{"p":"He nodded slowly. “Excellent system.”"},{"p":"“It’s supposed to make us ask questions.”"},{"p":"“Seems to be working.”"},{"p":"I was still irritated when Mom asked whether I wanted a ride to the academy. She’d been working an early shift all week and looked as though she’d slept inside her office chair."},{"p":"“Dad’s taking me,” I said."},{"p":"“Good. Your uncle called. He’ll come by Sunday if you want to show him your latest airplane drawings.”"},{"p":"“They’re route sketches, not drawings.”"},{"p":"“Of course,” she said. “Very serious lines.”"},{"p":"At the academy, the classroom smelled like dry-erase markers and the burnt popcorn someone had made in the break room. Rafe claimed it was an experiment in thermal convection. Beckett said the experiment had failed and should be removed from the building."},{"p":"Mara had pinned our hypothetical flight plan to the board. Beside it was a blank sheet labeled DECISION POINTS."},{"p":"“Before we start,” she said, “who found something missing?”"},{"p":"Every hand went up, including hers."},{"p":"“Good. A plan with missing information isn’t a puzzle where you guess the answer. It’s a reason to gather more information.”"},{"p":"We worked in pairs. Beckett and I were assigned the aircraft and pilot information. Imani and Rafe had the weather. I wasn’t sure whether Mara had paired us on purpose. She’d been doing that sort of thing lately: putting people together who could manage each other without setting anything on fire."},{"p":"Our sample aircraft was a Cessna 172S. The paperwork listed a reported fuel quantity and planned lesson duration, but no completed calculation of expected fuel use and remaining reserve. Beckett spotted it first."},{"p":"“We can’t claim two hours of fuel just because the gauges look half full,” he said."},{"p":"“Right. We need the actual fuel quantity and the planned burn.”"},{"p":"“And the required reserve,” he added."},{"p":"I looked up the applicable daytime VFR fuel-reserve concept in the reference Mara had provided. For an airplane operating under daytime VFR, the federal minimum is enough fuel to fly to the first point of intended landing and then, assuming normal cruising speed, for at least thirty more minutes. Mara had warned us that legal minimum fuel was not automatically a sensible planning target."},{"p":"“Thirty minutes doesn’t sound like much if you have to change plans,” I said."},{"p":"“It isn’t a magic shield,” Beckett replied. “It’s a minimum.”"},{"p":"Across the table, Imani had three weather reports arranged in time order. Rafe kept moving one of them to the front because its visibility number was highest."},{"p":"“Stop sorting by the nicest weather,” she told him. “Sort by when it happened.”"},{"p":"“I’m an optimist.”"},{"p":"“You’re making the forecast go backward.”"},{"p":"When Mara asked for our recommendation, I started with the list of missing information. The weather had been trending downward. We needed a current briefing, the expected timing of the changes, usable alternatives, and an honest look at whether a short local lesson offered enough flexibility."},{"p":"“So,” Mara said, “go or no-go?”"},{"p":"“I don’t know yet,” I said."},{"p":"“That’s not a recommendation.”"},{"p":"My ears warmed. “Then I’d recommend we don’t launch until we have the missing information. And if the uncertainty stays this high, we reschedule.”"},{"p":"“Better.”"},{"p":"Rafe raised his hand. “What if the student has been waiting three weeks?”"},{"p":"“That makes the disappointment bigger,” Mara said. “Does it improve the weather?”"},{"p":"“No. But I feel like it should.”"},{"p":"She smiled and pointed to the blank sheet. “Write down a decision point. Something that would make you stop, reassess, or change the plan.”"},{"p":"We proposed a latest departure time, a weather check before takeoff, and a point in the lesson for the instructor to reassess whether to continue or return. Mara was careful to remind us that these were planning ideas for a fictional exercise, not a substitute for actual flight rules, the pilot’s qualifications, or the aircraft’s limitations."},{"p":"“Who makes the final call in a real training flight?” she asked."},{"p":"“The pilot in command,” I said. “The instructor, if they’re acting as PIC.”"},{"p":"“And can the student speak up?”"},{"p":"“They’d better,” Imani said before I could answer."},{"p":"Mara tapped the board. “A good cockpit is not a place where somebody stays quiet because they think their concern sounds stupid.”"},{"p":"I looked down at my packet. The question at the bottom had seemed enormous yesterday. Now it looked different. Not easier, exactly. More useful."},{"p":"After class, Imani waited while I put my papers away."},{"p":"“Grandma’s appointment is Friday,” she said."},{"p":"“I remember.”"},{"p":"“I might not answer messages that day.”"},{"p":"“Okay.”"},{"p":"She watched me zip my bag. “You’re getting very good at saying okay.”"},{"p":"“It’s taking all my strength.”"},{"p":"“I can tell.”"},{"p":"Outside, a small airplane passed overhead, too far away for me to read its registration. Beckett guessed the model anyway. Rafe guessed something completely different, then claimed he had meant a different airplane."},{"p":"I opened my notebook in the car and crossed out my last question. Under it, I wrote another."},{"p":"What would make us change our minds?"},{"p":"I liked that one better. It sounded like something you could actually use."}]},{"t":"Chapter Eighteen — The Weather on the Other Side","blocks":[{"p":"Saturday sunlight revealed every streak on our windows that Friday had kindly hidden."},{"p":"Mom was scrubbing a pan in the sink when I came downstairs. She had a blue dish towel thrown over one shoulder and the expression she wore when the kitchen had personally insulted her."},{"p":"“Your instructor called,” she said."},{"p":"My stomach tightened. “Did she cancel?”"},{"p":"“She said to check your messages. Apparently you’re old enough to read a phone but not old enough to remember where you put it.”"},{"p":"I found it beneath a couch cushion. Mara had sent the academy group a short note: Sunday’s scheduled flight lessons were still tentative. Weather was expected to change, and we’d review the latest conditions before making any decisions. No promises."},{"p":"I suspected the last sentence had my name on it."},{"p":"By afternoon, the sky had gone pale and flat. The neighborhood was still bright, but the sunlight had lost its edges. I took my notebook to Uncle Everett’s house, where he was repairing a radio older than Dad."},{"p":"“You look like somebody told you the weather doesn’t care about your plans,” he said."},{"p":"“It doesn’t.”"},{"p":"“Excellent. You’ve learned something.”"},{"p":"He handed me a tiny screw to hold while he adjusted the radio’s back panel. His workbench smelled of solder and warm, dusty electronics."},{"p":"I told him about our scenario and the question Mara had made us answer. He listened without interrupting, which was one of his better tricks."},{"p":"“Did you ever have a flight you wanted to take that you couldn’t?” I asked."},{"p":"“More than one.”"},{"p":"“Did you get angry?”"},{"p":"“Usually. Sometimes at the weather. Sometimes at the schedule. Once at a vending machine that kept my crackers.”"},{"p":"“The crackers seem fair.”"},{"p":"“They were expensive crackers.”"},{"p":"He tightened a screw and set the radio upright. “I remember one day when the weather at our destination was getting worse. We had other options, and there were people whose job it was to help us plan and make decisions. We didn’t turn it into a contest to see who could be most determined.”"},{"p":"“What happened?”"},{"p":"“We changed the plan. Everybody complained. Everybody ate dinner somewhere they hadn’t expected.”"},{"p":"“That’s it?”"},{"p":"“That’s most of aviation, Lincoln. Not every good decision comes with a dramatic story attached.”"},{"p":"He switched on the radio. Static rattled through the speaker, followed by a faint voice reporting a baseball score. Everett looked pleased, though I wasn’t sure whether he was happy about the radio or the score."},{"p":"My phone buzzed. It was a message from Imani."},{"p":"*Grandma’s home. She’s tired. We still don’t know everything.*"},{"p":"I read it twice. I typed three different replies and deleted them all. Finally I wrote: *Glad she’s home. You don’t have to explain anything else. Want me to send a picture of Beckett’s sandwich tomorrow?*"},{"p":"She answered almost immediately."},{"p":"*Only if it’s worse than the last one.*"},{"p":"I smiled before I could stop myself."},{"p":"“Good news?” Everett asked."},{"p":"“Some.”"},{"p":"“Good.”"},{"p":"Sunday morning brought a low gray ceiling and a wet smell drifting off the pavement at Hampton Roads Executive Airport. I could hear an engine running somewhere beyond the hangars, but the far end of the field was partly hidden behind a curtain of mist."},{"p":"Mara met us inside. She’d already reviewed the latest weather and told the school staff that our flight would not proceed as planned. She explained that the observed and forecast conditions did not offer the margin she wanted for our visual training lesson. She didn’t pretend it was an emergency. She didn’t ask us to vote."},{"p":"“We’re staying on the ground,” she said."},{"p":"I had expected that. I was still disappointed."},{"p":"“Can we see what changed?” I asked."},{"p":"“That’s the lesson today.”"},{"p":"She brought up the current observations and forecasts, showing us how the weather had developed differently from the neat version we’d pictured two days earlier. The reports weren’t wrong just because conditions had changed. They described what was observed at specific places and times, while forecasts described what might happen later."},{"p":"Beckett leaned close to the screen. “So the forecast wasn’t a promise.”"},{"p":"“Correct.”"},{"p":"“I hate that,” Rafe said."},{"p":"“You hate uncertainty?” Mara asked."},{"p":"“I hate being told there’s a chance of something. It means I have to prepare for both things.”"},{"p":"“Now you’re starting to sound like a pilot.”"},{"p":"She asked us to build two plans on the board: one for a day when conditions remained comfortably suitable, and another for a day like this one. We could work on navigation, review weather products, inspect an aircraft with supervision, or practice radio phraseology on the ground. None of those required pretending the weather had improved."},{"p":"Imani chose radio practice because Rafe had been bragging about how good he was at it. He proceeded to mix up two call signs and then accused the imaginary air traffic controller of mumbling."},{"p":"“The controller is me,” Mara said."},{"p":"“Exactly. You understand my concern.”"},{"p":"We were laughing when a younger academy visitor appeared at the doorway. He held a model airplane with one wing bent upward. His father stood behind him, looking apologetic."},{"p":"“He wanted to ask whether airplanes can fly through clouds,” the father said."},{"p":"The boy looked at me, probably because I was nearest the door. “Can they?”"},{"p":"I thought about giving him the short answer. Yes, some aircraft and pilots can operate in clouds under the right rules and conditions. But I remembered how many questions I’d had at his age, and how annoying it felt when an adult gave me half an answer."},{"p":"“Some can,” I said. “But the pilot needs the right training, the airplane needs the right equipment, and there are rules about when it’s allowed. When you’re flying in a cloud, you can’t just look outside to tell which way is up.”"},{"p":"He frowned. “But birds do it.”"},{"p":"“Birds have an unfair advantage,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“They also don’t file flight plans,” Rafe added."},{"p":"Mara stepped beside us. “Even a trained pilot with the right equipment has to make careful decisions about weather. Clouds can hide other hazards, and thunderstorms are dangerous even for very capable airplanes.”"},{"p":"The boy considered this. Then he held up his bent model. “Can you fix this?”"},{"p":"Beckett was already reaching for it. “Now that is a question with an answer.”"},{"p":"We spent the next ten minutes straightening the wing and testing the model in the hallway, with Mara’s permission and a strict rule about keeping it away from the office door. The boy launched it too hard, and it struck the wall with a papery smack."},{"p":"“I think it needs a better pilot,” he said."},{"p":"“Join the club,” I told him."},{"p":"When Dad arrived to pick me up, the mist was lifting, but the sky still looked heavy. He asked whether we’d flown."},{"p":"“No.”"},{"p":"“Bad day?”"},{"p":"I watched Beckett and Rafe argue beside the hangar over who had repaired the model’s wing correctly. Imani was standing between them, holding the airplane out of reach until they stopped grabbing at it."},{"p":"“Not really,” I said."},{"p":"Dad followed my gaze. “That’s new.”"},{"p":"“A little.”"},{"p":"On the drive home, I checked the weather again. The numbers had improved at the airport. I felt a familiar pinch of annoyance. If we’d waited another hour, maybe we could have flown. Or maybe the conditions still wouldn’t have been suitable for the lesson. I didn’t know."},{"p":"I put the phone away before I could turn that uncertainty into a complaint."},{"p":"Tomorrow’s forecast showed a picture of a sun behind a cloud. I left it alone."}]},{"t":"Chapter Nineteen — The Space Between Two Reports","blocks":[{"p":"A plastic trophy shaped like a cloud was waiting on Mara’s desk."},{"p":"It sat crooked on its base, with a silver sticker that read WEATHER CHAMPION. One of the cloud’s edges had broken off. Rafe picked it up, turned it around, and inspected the back."},{"p":"“This is terrible,” he said. “I want it.”"},{"p":"“You can’t have it,” Mara told him. “It’s not yours.”"},{"p":"“Yet.”"},{"p":"We had a Saturday workshop instead of a flight. Mara had promised a weather challenge, and Rafe had spent the whole week announcing that he was going to win. He’d even changed his group-chat name to Captain Cumulus. Nobody had asked him to do that."},{"p":"Imani arrived carrying two notebooks and a bag of pretzels. Her braids were tucked into the collar of her jacket, and she had the exhausted look of someone who’d been awake before the alarm. She’d told me her grandmother was having a better week. I didn’t ask whether that meant better than last week or better than the doctors had expected. I was getting used to leaving some questions alone."},{"p":"Beckett came in behind her with a cardboard box."},{"p":"“Is that lunch?” Rafe asked."},{"p":"“It’s a weather station.”"},{"p":"“So no.”"},{"p":"Beckett opened the box. Inside were a thermometer, a cheap barometer, two paper cups mounted on crossed straws, and enough tape to repair a small boat. He’d made a cup anemometer, or something that resembled one after being stepped on."},{"p":"“It spins,” he said, which seemed to be his main defense."},{"p":"Mara gave us each a printed packet. At the top was a fictional route between two airports, with an estimated departure time, an alternate airport, and a note that the pilot wanted to take a family member to an afternoon event. Below that were weather observations and forecasts."},{"p":"“Every report has a time,” Mara said. “Every location has a name. Read both before you decide anything. And remember, this is a classroom exercise. None of you are being asked to authorize an actual flight.”"},{"p":"The first observation showed good visibility at the departure airport. The second showed lowering clouds at the destination. A forecast suggested conditions might improve later. Farther down the page, an amended forecast changed that prediction."},{"p":"I noticed the amendment immediately. Last year I might have circled it and waited for Mara to praise me. Instead I checked its issue time, then went back to the observation above it."},{"p":"“This one came out after the forecast,” I said."},{"p":"“Good,” Mara replied. “What does that tell you?”"},{"p":"“That the forecast wasn’t the most recently issued item in the packet.”"},{"p":"“Does it tell you what the weather is right now?”"},{"p":"I looked again. The observation was from forty minutes earlier. “No.”"},{"p":"Rafe leaned over. “The clouds probably got bored and left.”"},{"p":"“That is not an approved forecast method,” Imani said."},{"p":"“It should be.”"},{"p":"We split into pairs. Imani and I took the destination and alternate. Beckett and Rafe took the departure airport and the route. Mara made us write down what we knew, what we thought might happen, and what we still needed to check. The third column was the longest."},{"p":"I didn’t love that."},{"p":"At first the exercise seemed easy: wait for the destination to improve, then go. But the event had a start time, and the flight would take longer than the drive from one end of Virginia Beach to the other. If we waited too long, the imaginary passenger would miss the thing they were traveling for."},{"p":"“Then the trip might not make sense,” Imani said."},{"p":"“It could still work if the weather improves early.”"},{"p":"“Could.”"},{"p":"“I’m allowed to say could.”"},{"p":"“You’re allowed to say whatever you want. You just have to put it in the right column.”"},{"p":"She pointed to POSSIBLE, not KNOWN, with the end of her pencil."},{"p":"I moved my sentence. I did not enjoy how satisfying she found that."},{"p":"Mara handed out another sheet. The destination’s latest observation was worse than before. The alternate still looked usable in the exercise, but a band of showers was developing along part of the proposed route."},{"p":"“New information,” she said. “You have four minutes.”"},{"p":"Rafe slapped the table. “Four minutes? I need to consult my legal team.”"},{"p":"“Your legal team is Beckett,” Mara said."},{"p":"“Then I need a different legal team.”"},{"p":"We studied the reports. Imani underlined the observation times. I drew a line across the route on the map and immediately regretted it because the pen went straight through a town name."},{"p":"“Don’t scribble over the place we’re trying to find,” she said."},{"p":"“It’s a fictional trip. The town will recover.”"},{"p":"“The town would appreciate a readable chart.”"},{"p":"I laughed, then stopped when I saw the expression on her face. She wasn’t joking about the chart. We were still learning where the line was between teasing and ignoring each other."},{"p":"“Here,” I said, sliding the map over. “Use the clean copy.”"},{"p":"She took the clean map and went back to work."},{"p":"Our answer was to delay and reassess. If the weather didn’t improve enough to meet the pilot’s established limits with a comfortable margin, the trip wouldn’t happen by air. We listed driving as an option, though it might mean arriving late."},{"p":"When Mara asked for our reasoning, Imani did most of the talking. I added that the alternate was not a magic escape hatch; it had its own weather, distance, and planning requirements. Beckett nodded from the other table. I couldn’t tell whether he was agreeing or checking that the trophy was still on the desk."},{"p":"Mara listened, then asked the question I’d been waiting for."},{"p":"“Would you launch now?”"},{"p":"“No,” I said."},{"p":"She waited."},{"p":"“Not on this information,” I added. “And not just because the destination’s bad. We’d be trying to make the weather fit a schedule.”"},{"p":"“All right.”"},{"p":"That was it. No dramatic music. No trophy presentation."},{"p":"Rafe raised his hand. “Who wins?”"},{"p":"“Nobody wins a real weather decision,” Mara said. “In this exercise, both teams identified important problems.”"},{"p":"“That cloud has been sitting there all morning for nothing?”"},{"p":"“The cloud is a paperweight.”"},{"p":"He stared at it. “I’ve been emotionally misled.”"},{"p":"After the workshop, we carried Beckett’s homemade weather station outside. The sky was a pale, washed-out blue, and a breeze pushed dry leaves along the fence. His paper cups turned in uneven circles. Sometimes they stopped, then started again when a stronger puff reached them."},{"p":"“See?” he said. “Works.”"},{"p":"“How fast is the wind?” I asked."},{"p":"“Fast enough to spin it.”"},{"p":"“Excellent precision.”"},{"p":"“It’s a prototype.”"},{"p":"Imani held up her phone to compare the airport’s reported wind with what we felt in the parking lot. Mara had reminded us that a homemade instrument near buildings couldn’t replace an official observation. The cups were sheltered whenever the wind came from the hangars, and the readings weren’t calibrated anyway."},{"p":"“So the station is mostly decoration,” Rafe said."},{"p":"“The station,” Beckett replied, “is mostly a reason to build a better station.”"},{"p":"I looked at the spinning cups. They were clumsy and made a small ticking sound every time one straw brushed the box. Still, they showed something the paper reports hadn’t: the air moving right where we stood, at that particular second."},{"p":"The paper cups spun, stalled, and spun again. I watched them longer than I needed to."},{"p":"Imani nudged the box with her shoe. “Your bearing’s rubbing.”"},{"p":"Beckett crouched. “I know.”"},{"p":"“You said it worked.”"},{"p":"“It does work. Badly. That’s different.”"},{"p":"I watched him peel off a piece of tape and adjust the straw. Rafe offered to hold the box, then immediately tilted it sideways to make the cups spin faster."},{"p":"“You’re adding wind,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“I’m improving the atmosphere.”"},{"p":"“Put it down.”"},{"p":"I held the box steady while Beckett fixed the rubbing straw. It took longer than he expected. Nobody told him to hurry."}]},{"t":"Chapter Twenty — The Window Seat","blocks":[{"p":"On Monday, Mom got home from work with a cardboard tube under her arm and a headache behind her eyes."},{"p":"“Please tell me that’s not another poster for the refrigerator,” Dad said."},{"p":"“It’s a map,” she answered."},{"p":"“That is exactly what the last poster was.”"},{"p":"She unrolled it across the kitchen table. It was a street map from her office, the kind with thin blue lines for waterways and blocks of color for neighborhoods. One corner kept curling toward the salt shaker."},{"p":"“My office replaced these,” she said. “I thought Lincoln might want the old one for his wall.”"},{"p":"I did want it. I tried not to look too pleased, which was pointless because Mom saw everything except the socks I left behind the couch."},{"p":"“Thanks,” I said."},{"p":"“You’re welcome. Please don’t use it to plan an actual flight.”"},{"p":"“I was going to tape it over my bed.”"},{"p":"“That’s fine. If you fly your bed anywhere, tell me first.”"},{"p":"Dad set down a pot of pasta. “Our homeowners insurance may not cover that.”"},{"p":"We ate with the map still taking up half the table. Mom had to move the bread basket twice to keep it from rolling itself shut. I traced the roads toward Chesapeake with one finger and found the airport. It looked tiny from above, just a few shapes among roads and fields."},{"p":"“How’s Imani?” Mom asked."},{"p":"“Better, I think. Her grandma’s home.”"},{"p":"“That’s good.”"},{"p":"I poked at a piece of pasta. “I messed up a while ago. I told somebody something I wasn’t supposed to.”"},{"p":"Mom waited. She was good at waiting when she wanted an answer and terrible at it when she wanted us to leave for the grocery store."},{"p":"“It was about her grandmother. I thought I was helping.”"},{"p":"“Was she angry?”"},{"p":"“Yes.”"},{"p":"“Are you surprised?”"},{"p":"“Not anymore.”"},{"p":"Dad reached for the bread. He didn’t jump in. I was grateful for that, although I also wanted somebody to tell me the problem was finished."},{"p":"“I apologized,” I said. “We’re talking again. It’s still different.”"},{"p":"Mom twisted her fork slowly. “It might be for a while.”"},{"p":"“How long?”"},{"p":"“I don’t know.”"},{"p":"I hated that answer. I was also beginning to recognize it as one people gave when they were telling the truth."},{"p":"The next afternoon, the academy had an optional ground session. I almost skipped it. Theo had invited me to shoot baskets, and I was tired of being the kid who always had a weather report open. But Beckett had promised to bring the repaired weather station, and Imani had texted that she would be there."},{"p":"Dad drove me after school. A thin layer of cloud covered most of the sky. The sun was visible through it as a pale white spot, like somebody holding a flashlight behind a bedsheet."},{"p":"“Looks harmless,” Dad said."},{"p":"“Might be.”"},{"p":"He glanced over. “That’s a very unsatisfying answer.”"},{"p":"“I’ve been practicing.”"},{"p":"At the academy, Mara had set a small table by the window with a weather chart, a blank flight-planning form, and a pair of binoculars. Mara explained that the binoculars were for identifying distant landmarks from the ground—not for determining official visibility."},{"p":"“Today we’re comparing what you see with what the reports say,” she told us. “And then we’re going to talk about what neither can tell you.”"},{"p":"Rafe stood at the window and pointed toward a line of trees. “I can see those.”"},{"p":"“How far away are they?” Mara asked."},{"p":"“Tree distance.”"},{"p":"“Try again.”"},{"p":"We used the map to estimate the distance to known landmarks. The view was hazy but clear enough to pick out the nearer ones. Farther away, the horizon had gone soft. Mara reminded us that official prevailing visibility is determined using established observing procedures, not by a student guessing through a window."},{"p":"I wrote that down before I could confidently teach it wrong to somebody younger."},{"p":"Mara showed us two weather observations from different airports. One reported reduced visibility; the other was considerably better. The airports weren’t far apart on the map."},{"p":"“How can they both be right?” she asked."},{"p":"“Because they’re different places,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“And different times,” Imani added, checking the issue stamps."},{"p":"“Good. What else?”"},{"p":"I thought about the fog from my birthday week, how the water tower had disappeared from our street while the weather app insisted the whole city was sunny. “Weather isn’t one big blanket,” I said. “It can change over a few miles.”"},{"p":"Mara nodded. “And some changes happen quickly.”"},{"p":"She gave us a new scenario. A pilot had planned a short daytime visual flight. Departure conditions were acceptable at the time of the report, but the destination’s visibility had dropped. The pilot had a passenger worried about being late for a family event. There was no emergency, no special equipment, and no reason to believe the weather would improve on schedule."},{"p":"“You know the drill,” Mara said. “Tell me what you’d do, and tell me what you still need to know.”"},{"p":"I started writing. Then I stopped."},{"p":"“Is the passenger mad?” I asked."},{"p":"Rafe snorted. “Obviously. They’re late.”"},{"p":"“That’s not in the report,” I said."},{"p":"“You want an emotional METAR?”"},{"p":"“No. I’m wondering if we’re supposed to ignore the pressure.”"},{"p":"Mara rested one hand on the back of a chair. “You shouldn’t ignore that pressure exists. You also shouldn’t let it make the safety decision for you.”"},{"p":"The room got quiet for a moment. Outside, a small airplane taxied past the window, moving slowly enough that I could see the pilot turn their head to check the path ahead."},{"p":"I remembered how badly I’d wanted our flights to go ahead during the last two summers. Every cancellation had felt personal. I hadn’t thought much about how Mara had to make those decisions with an actual student sitting beside her, excited and disappointed and probably talking too much."},{"p":"We worked through the scenario. Imani listed the missing information. Beckett checked the distance to a suitable alternative airport. Rafe argued that the passenger should call the family and explain the delay, which wasn’t an aviation calculation but was probably useful. I wrote that the pilot should reassess with current information and be prepared to cancel or use ground transportation if conditions didn’t meet the pilot’s limits."},{"p":"When Mara collected our papers, she didn’t tell us which answer was the bravest. She asked whether we had allowed the passenger’s schedule to change the weather facts."},{"p":"Nobody changed the weather on our papers to make the passenger happy."},{"p":"Afterward, Imani and I were the last two students putting away chairs. Beckett and Rafe had gone outside to test the repaired anemometer, where Rafe was almost certainly doing something to make the results useless."},{"p":"“My grandma asked about you,” Imani said."},{"p":"I folded a chair and nearly caught my thumb in the hinge. “Did she?”"},{"p":"“She wanted to know if you’d learned to stop throwing paper airplanes into furniture.”"},{"p":"“That was one lamp. And it wasn’t me.”"},{"p":"“It was absolutely you.”"},{"p":"I set the chair against the wall. “How’s she doing?”"},{"p":"Imani looked toward the window. “Some days are better. Some aren’t. She gets annoyed when everyone asks how she’s feeling every six minutes.”"},{"p":"“That sounds familiar.”"},{"p":"“You are not my grandmother.”"},{"p":"“I was going to say it sounds like my dad when he has a cold.”"},{"p":"“That’s worse.”"},{"p":"We carried the last chairs to the storage rack. There was a long pause, not the comfortable kind, but not terrible either."},{"p":"“I haven’t told anybody else,” I said. “About what happened.”"},{"p":"She looked at me. “Good.”"},{"p":"I wanted to say more. I wanted to explain that I understood now, that I’d learned my lesson, that I would never do it again. Those were all things I hoped were true. None of them would make the earlier mistake disappear."},{"p":"“I should’ve asked you first,” I said."},{"p":"“Yeah,” she replied. “You should’ve.”"},{"p":"Outside, somebody yelled, and we both turned. Rafe was chasing a paper cup across the grass while Beckett shouted that he was ruining the calibration."},{"p":"Imani smiled despite herself. “They need adult supervision.”"},{"p":"“They have it. It isn’t working.”"},{"p":"We walked out together. She didn’t forgive me all over again, and I didn’t ask her to. Beckett’s weather station had tipped onto its side, and one of the straws was bent at an angle that looked expensive to repair in terms of tape."},{"p":"Rafe held up the escaped cup like he’d caught a wild animal."},{"p":"“Wind speed,” he announced, “is faster than me.”"},{"p":"“That’s not a high bar,” I said."},{"p":"Beckett took the cup back. “Nobody touch anything.”"},{"p":"Imani knelt beside him and held the box steady. I crouched on the other side, trying to keep the wind from lifting the loose paper on top. The four of us stayed there until the cups were turning again, though the repaired instrument still clicked once every revolution."},{"p":"I thought about pointing that out."},{"p":"Instead I asked Beckett whether he wanted another piece of tape."}]},{"t":"Chapter Twenty-One — The Envelope Under the Wiper","blocks":[{"p":"The envelope was wedged beneath Dad’s windshield wiper when we came out of the grocery store."},{"p":"Dad stopped beside the car with a bag of oranges balanced against his hip and made the face he usually made when the printer at the library refused to print."},{"p":"“Parking ticket?” I asked."},{"p":"“In a grocery store parking lot?”"},{"p":"“I’m trying to prepare you for the worst.”"},{"p":"It wasn’t a ticket. It was a flyer for a neighborhood science fair, damp around the edges from the afternoon mist. Dad held it away from his jacket as though it might drip on him."},{"p":"“You could enter,” he said. “They’ve got a weather category.”"},{"p":"“I’ve already helped Theo build one weather thing. The neighborhood doesn’t need another wind vane made from a drinking straw.”"},{"p":"“This one has prizes.”"},{"p":"“How much?”"},{"p":"“First prize is a gift certificate.”"},{"p":"“For what?”"},{"p":"Dad read the tiny print. “A garden center.”"},{"p":"I opened the trunk. “My retirement plan is taking shape.”"},{"p":"At home, Mom had spread folders across the kitchen table. She was looking for a property record somebody at work had insisted existed in three different places. The folders smelled faintly of dust. Dad dropped an orange peel into the trash."},{"p":"“You’re home early,” she said."},{"p":"“The grocery store ran out of reasons to keep us,” Dad replied."},{"p":"Mom’s phone buzzed. She read the screen, frowned, and turned it facedown."},{"p":"“Everything okay?” I asked."},{"p":"“A work thing.”"},{"p":"I waited. Mom went back to the folders."},{"p":"I almost asked what kind of work thing. Instead I carried the milk to the refrigerator. A few months earlier I might have kept asking until she told me or got annoyed enough to send me upstairs. I’d learned that being curious didn’t mean every answer belonged to me."},{"p":"That didn’t make it easier not to ask."},{"p":"The next morning at the academy, Mara had taped an envelope to the whiteboard. On the front she’d written: OPEN ONLY AFTER YOU MAKE A DECISION."},{"p":"Rafe stared at it. “That’s either a weather exercise or a very disappointing birthday card.”"},{"p":"“Both are possible,” Mara said."},{"p":"On the table were two flight-planning packets. Both described the same proposed daytime training flight from Hampton Roads Executive Airport. The first packet contained current observations, a forecast, a broad weather briefing, and a route sketched on a chart. The second was sealed with a paper clip."},{"p":"“You’re not the pilot today,” Mara reminded us. “You’re a team of students preparing a recommendation. The actual instructor and pilot would make the real flight decision.”"},{"p":"“So we can’t crash the imaginary airplane,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“You can certainly make an imaginary bad decision.”"},{"p":"That was less comforting."},{"p":"The departure weather was fine for the fictional flight. The destination was reporting improving visibility, but the forecast showed a chance of low clouds returning. Along part of the route, the temperature and dew point were close. Imani circled the two numbers and put a question mark beside them."},{"p":"“Those being close doesn’t automatically mean fog,” she said, “but I want to know whether visibility is likely to get worse.”"},{"p":"“We could go now and beat it,” Rafe suggested."},{"p":"“Beat what?” I asked."},{"p":"“The low clouds.”"},{"p":"“We don’t know exactly when they’ll show up.”"},{"p":"Rafe tapped the chart. “That’s why I said could.”"},{"p":"I could feel an argument trying to get started. Mara stood by the window, saying nothing. Outside, a mechanic rolled a cart across the apron, one wheel squeaking every few feet."},{"p":"We looked through the packet again. The proposed flight was a lesson, not a trip anyone had to make. We could move it."},{"p":"“What are our options?” Imani asked."},{"p":"“Delay and get updated weather,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“Change the lesson to ground school,” I said."},{"p":"“Go somewhere else if the instructor thinks that’s appropriate,” Rafe added."},{"p":"We wrote down our recommendation: delay the proposed flight, get updated weather, and review the plan with the instructor."},{"p":"Mara nodded toward the envelope."},{"p":"“Go ahead.”"},{"p":"I pulled it off the board. Inside was a single sheet with a new destination observation. The reported visibility had fallen, and the ceiling was lower than before. The fictional flight would have been approaching the area around the time those conditions developed."},{"p":"“So we were right,” I said."},{"p":"Mara held up one finger. “Careful.”"},{"p":"I lowered the paper."},{"p":"“You made a decision using the information available at the time,” she said. “That’s what I want you to evaluate. If the weather had improved instead, would waiting automatically have become the wrong choice?”"},{"p":"I looked at the sheet again. “No.”"},{"p":"“Why not?”"},{"p":"“Because we didn’t know that when we decided.”"},{"p":"She nodded and went to erase the board."},{"p":"I understood. Mostly. I still wanted the paper to say CONGRATULATIONS in enormous letters, preferably beneath a trophy. Instead it was just a report from an imaginary airport telling us that the weather had gotten worse."},{"p":"At lunch we sat outside on a low concrete wall near the academy building. Beckett had brought a sandwich in a container that looked capable of surviving a car accident. Rafe had bought something from a vending machine that made a crunching sound when he shook the bag."},{"p":"“You know what I hate?” Rafe asked."},{"p":"“Several things,” Imani said."},{"p":"“When you study for a test and the question isn’t about the thing you studied.”"},{"p":"“That’s every test you’ve ever taken,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“Exactly. I have a consistent complaint.”"},{"p":"I took a bite of my sandwich. Across the apron, a Cessna was being pushed into position by two adults. Its wings moved slightly as a breeze crossed the pavement."},{"p":"“I wanted the second report to prove we’d done something smart,” I said."},{"p":"Imani peeled the label off her water bottle. “It didn’t have to.”"},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"“Do you?”"},{"p":"I looked at her. She wasn’t smiling, but she wasn’t trying to embarrass me either."},{"p":"“I’m working on it,” I said."},{"p":"She stuck the label to the side of her lunch bag. “Good. Because the weather doesn’t hand out medals for being cautious.”"},{"p":"“It should,” Rafe said. “Little cloud-shaped ones.”"},{"p":"“Yours would say MOST LIKELY TO ARGUE WITH A RADAR IMAGE,” Beckett told him."},{"p":"“I’d wear it.”"},{"p":"After lunch, Mara showed us how to build a simple personal-minimums worksheet. Mara reminded us that a worksheet could never replace regulations, aircraft limitations, or instructor requirements. Personal minimums were additional limits, not substitutes for the rules."},{"p":"“Think of it as making some decisions before you feel rushed,” she said."},{"p":"I wrote that down in the margin of my notebook, then crossed out the words some decisions. Not everything could be settled in advance. Weather could change. People could change their minds. You could miss a detail and have to start over."},{"p":"I rewrote the sentence: Decide what you can early. Check the rest again."},{"p":"When Dad picked me up, the science-fair flyer was still on the dashboard, curling in the sun."},{"p":"“Any interest?” he asked."},{"p":"“Maybe.”"},{"p":"“That’s not a yes.”"},{"p":"“I’ve been practicing.”"},{"p":"He laughed and pulled out of the parking space. The envelope from Mara’s exercise was folded in my backpack. I hadn’t kept it as proof that I was right. I wanted to show Theo how the exercise worked."},{"p":"Though I might draw a little cloud-shaped medal on the back first."}]},{"t":"Chapter Twenty-Two — The Day We Stayed","blocks":[{"p":"On Saturday morning, the sky was clear enough to see the little airplane-shaped weather vane on the neighbor’s shed. From the street, its tail made it look as if it pointed the wrong way. I had to remind myself that its nose pointed into the wind."},{"p":"I checked the forecast before breakfast, then again while the toaster warmed up."},{"p":"“If you stare at it long enough,” Dad said, “will it improve?”"},{"p":"“That’s not how forecasts work.”"},{"p":"“I was hoping you’d invented a useful new method.”"},{"p":"I was supposed to fly with Mara that afternoon. The lesson had been on the calendar for three weeks, shifted twice, and nearly canceled once because the airplane needed a routine inspection. I wanted it badly enough that I had already pictured the climb out, the shoreline in the distance, the instruments sitting where they belonged."},{"p":"By eleven, the updated forecast showed a greater chance of showers and thunderstorms during the afternoon. The weather at the airport was still good. That made the decision harder to understand, not easier."},{"p":"At the academy, Rafe met me at the door holding two paper cups of hot chocolate. One had a lid that wouldn’t stay on."},{"p":"“I bought you this,” he said, “because I figured we’d either celebrate flying or need something to complain into.”"},{"p":"“How much do I owe you?”"},{"p":"“You can pay me back by holding the lid.”"},{"p":"Inside, Mara had a chart open on the computer. She wasn’t wearing her headset. That told me more than I wanted it to."},{"p":"“We’re not flying,” I said."},{"p":"“I haven’t made the final call yet,” she answered. “Come look.”"},{"p":"I put down the cup. She pointed to the latest observations, the forecast, and the radar display. The radar image had a timestamp in the corner. Recent images showed showers developing west of the airport, and the forecast called for possible thunderstorms during our planned lesson window."},{"p":"“Could we move the lesson earlier?” I asked."},{"p":"“We could consider it. But we still need time to brief, inspect the airplane, taxi, fly the lesson, and return safely. What else matters?”"},{"p":"“How fast the weather is moving,” I said."},{"p":"“And?”"},{"p":"“Whether it might build somewhere we can’t see from here.”"},{"p":"“Keep going.”"},{"p":"I looked at the chart. The airport had a perfectly ordinary patch of blue sky above it. I’d never disliked blue sky quite so much."},{"p":"“We don’t have to go,” I said."},{"p":"Mara leaned back. “That’s true.”"},{"p":"“I don’t want that to be the answer.”"},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"She waited while I looked again. There were questions I couldn’t answer from a weather app. I couldn’t promise the clouds would stay where they were. I couldn’t guarantee we’d be back before the forecast changed. Wanting to fly didn’t change any of that."},{"p":"“I think we should stay,” I said."},{"p":"Mara studied me for a second. “I agree. I’ll make the cancellation.”"},{"p":"The disappointment arrived anyway. It felt physical, like missing the last step on a staircase. I nodded as if I’d expected it all along and walked outside before I said something stupid."},{"p":"Beckett was by the fence, tightening the strap on his backpack. He saw my face and didn’t ask whether we’d flown."},{"p":"“No luck?”"},{"p":"“No flight.”"},{"p":"He moved aside so I could lean on the rail. Beyond it, a windsock hung at an angle in the light breeze. A small airplane taxied past, headed toward a hangar. Somewhere behind us a door slammed."},{"p":"“At least you didn’t get halfway through and have to turn around,” Beckett said."},{"p":"“I know.”"},{"p":"“That was supposed to make you feel better.”"},{"p":"“It didn’t.”"},{"p":"“Okay.”"},{"p":"We stood there without talking. That was new for us. Usually one of us would have filled the silence with a correction or a joke that didn’t quite land."},{"p":"After a while, I said, “I thought being better at this would mean I’d be less disappointed.”"},{"p":"“Why?”"},{"p":"“I don’t know. Because I’d understand it.”"},{"p":"Beckett adjusted his glasses. “Understanding why my bike chain broke doesn’t make me happy about walking home.”"},{"p":"“That’s a terrible comparison.”"},{"p":"“It’s an excellent comparison. You just don’t like it.”"},{"p":"He was probably right, which was annoying."},{"p":"Back inside, Mara gave us an option. We could leave, or we could help prepare a weather workshop for the younger academy visitors arriving that afternoon. Rafe chose the workshop immediately because he heard the words dry ice and got excited before Mara explained that we would not be using any."},{"p":"“I was robbed,” he said."},{"p":"We set up a table with clear plastic containers, printed cloud photographs, a thermometer, and a simple map. The activity was about comparing observations, not manufacturing dramatic weather. We made cards showing three fictional airports at different times. Visitors had to decide which information was current and what they would want to know next."},{"p":"Imani wrote the questions. Beckett assembled a cardboard display with little sliding windows. Rafe drew clouds that looked like mashed potatoes."},{"p":"I drew a thunderstorm. He looked at it and said, “Why does yours have an angry face?”"},{"p":"“It doesn’t.”"},{"p":"“It has eyebrows.”"},{"p":"“Those are rain bands.”"},{"p":"“Sure.”"},{"p":"By the time the first visitors arrived, the room smelled like markers and somebody’s popcorn. A girl about ten years old came over with her grandfather. She read our first card, then pointed at the map."},{"p":"“If it’s sunny here, why can’t they fly there?”"},{"p":"I opened my mouth with a complete explanation ready. It involved ceilings, visibility, and a sentence about local conditions that would have taken most of the afternoon."},{"p":"The girl looked at the wall clock and then at the cards again."},{"p":"I closed my mouth."},{"p":"“What time does this report say?” I asked."},{"p":"She found the number in the corner. “Nine.”"},{"p":"“And this one?”"},{"p":"“Eleven.”"},{"p":"“Which is newer?”"},{"p":"“Eleven.”"},{"p":"“What’s different?”"},{"p":"She compared the cards, lips moving slightly as she read. “It got cloudy. And harder to see.”"},{"p":"“Right. The sky over our heads can’t tell us everything about somewhere else.”"},{"p":"She considered that. “So they need to check again.”"},{"p":"“Exactly.”"},{"p":"She smiled and slid the cardboard window across to reveal the next report. Her grandfather gave me a little nod. I tried not to look as pleased as I felt."},{"p":"Across the room, Imani was helping a boy who kept confusing wind direction with the direction an airplane would travel. Beckett’s sliding-window display stuck twice, and Rafe had to hold it in place while a visitor pulled the tab. Nobody seemed to mind."},{"p":"At three o’clock, rain began tapping the windows. A few minutes later it came down hard enough to blur the parked cars. We heard distant thunder. Mara kept everyone indoors and away from the windows and exterior doors while the thunderstorm passed."},{"p":"Rafe stood beside me, listening to the rain hammer the roof."},{"p":"“Well,” he said, “your forecast was pretty good.”"},{"p":"“It wasn’t mine.”"},{"p":"“You know what I mean.”"},{"p":"I did. Rain wasn’t a trophy for staying on the ground. We had made the call with the information we had, before the weather arrived."},{"p":"My phone buzzed. Mom had sent a message asking whether we were inside. I typed back that we were, and that I’d call when it was safe for Dad to come get me."},{"p":"Imani joined us near the display table. She was carrying the little girl’s completed weather card."},{"p":"“She wants to take this home,” she said. “I told her she could.”"},{"p":"“That’s fine.”"},{"p":"“You didn’t lecture her.”"},{"p":"“I can, if you want. I’ve got about fifteen minutes prepared.”"},{"p":"“Please don’t.”"},{"p":"She handed me the card. On the back, the girl had drawn a small airplane beneath a very large cloud. The airplane was parked on the ground. Beside it she’d written, in crooked letters: WAIT FOR BETTER WEATHER."},{"p":"I put the card carefully into my notebook. Rain hammered the roof. Beckett was arguing with Rafe about whether their cardboard display counted as weatherproof, and Mara was trying to find a towel for the water dripping from somebody’s umbrella."},{"p":"I still wished we’d flown."},{"p":"I was glad we hadn’t."},{"p":"Both things stayed true all the way home."}]},{"t":"Epilogue — The Clear Part of the Sky","blocks":[{"p":"The next Saturday, the sky over Virginia Beach was the sort of blue people put on postcards. Not a cloud above the baseball diamond. Not even one of those skinny white streaks that looked as though somebody had dragged a chalk-covered finger across the sky."},{"p":"Theo stood beside me in the outfield, picking grass out of his glove."},{"p":"“Perfect flying weather,” he said."},{"p":"“Maybe,” I said."},{"p":"He turned slowly. “Did you just say maybe to a cloudless sky?”"},{"p":"“I did.”"},{"p":"“You’re exhausting.”"},{"p":"“That’s what my mom says.”"},{"p":"He tossed the grass into the breeze. It went sideways, not straight down. The flag over the concession stand stirred, then dropped against its pole."},{"p":"“So what’s wrong with it?” he asked."},{"p":"“Nothing I can tell from here. That’s the point.”"},{"p":"“I regret asking.”"},{"p":"A batter sent a low line drive toward right field. I took three steps before I realized it was going to drop short. I ran harder, stretched out my glove, and missed it by a foot. The ball bounced past me. I chased it to the fence while the other team yelled themselves hoarse."},{"p":"Theo met me halfway back."},{"p":"“At least you didn’t stop to check the forecast,” he said."},{"p":"“I was considering it.”"},{"p":"“You should’ve considered running faster.”"},{"p":"Our coach called the next play. We went back to our spots, and for the next twenty minutes I thought mostly about baseball. It felt good to be bad at something ordinary for a change."},{"p":"After the game, Mom was waiting by the bleachers with a paper bag of oranges. Dad had a library book tucked under his arm, because he apparently couldn’t attend any event without bringing one. Uncle Everett leaned against the fence, wearing a faded cap with a bent brim."},{"p":"“Tough hop,” Everett said."},{"p":"“It wasn’t a hop. I missed it in the air.”"},{"p":"“Ah. Tough air.”"},{"p":"“You’re all hilarious today.”"},{"p":"Dad offered me an orange. I took two."},{"p":"My phone buzzed in my pocket. The academy group chat had acquired seventeen messages while I was playing. Most were Rafe trying to persuade Beckett to build a working miniature airport out of cereal boxes. Beckett had asked for a budget. Rafe had replied with a photograph of six dollars and a coupon for frozen yogurt."},{"p":"Then I saw a message from Imani, sent just a minute earlier."},{"p":"*Grandma wants to know if the cardboard weather window survived.*"},{"p":"I typed: *Mostly. Beckett says it needs a structural inspection.*"},{"p":"Three dots appeared, vanished, and returned."},{"p":"*She says tell him the old one worked fine and to stop improving it.*"},{"p":"I laughed loud enough that Mom looked over."},{"p":"“Everything all right?” she asked."},{"p":"“Yeah.”"},{"p":"That was true. Imani and I still had awkward moments. Sometimes I caught myself starting to ask about her grandmother and stopped, not because I didn’t care, but because I’d finally learned to let Imani decide how much she wanted to say. Other times she brought it up herself. Her grandmother still had appointments and got tired easily. There wasn’t a neat ending to that part of the summer."},{"p":"But there was a next conversation. And sometimes another one after that."},{"p":"We walked toward the parking lot. Everett fell into step beside me."},{"p":"“Mara says you’ve been asking better questions,” he said."},{"p":"“She said that?”"},{"p":"“She said you ask more questions. I added the better part.”"},{"p":"“Great. Thanks.”"},{"p":"He grinned, then grew quiet as we passed the chain-link gate."},{"p":"“You know, when I was your age, I thought confidence meant never having to admit I wasn’t sure.”"},{"p":"“You?” I said. “The guy who owns four radios that don’t work?”"},{"p":"“Five. One stopped working Thursday.”"},{"p":"I waited for him to turn it into a speech. Instead he bent down to pick up a bottle cap and dropped it in a trash can."},{"p":"“Did you ever get over it?” I asked."},{"p":"“Not completely,” he said. “Ask me again next year.”"},{"p":"We reached the car. Above the roofs, a small airplane crossed a patch of bright sky. I could hear its engine only after I spotted it. From where I stood, I couldn’t tell where it had departed, what its pilot had checked, or what the weather was doing along its route."},{"p":"I could only see this little piece of its flight."},{"p":"For once, I didn’t try to make up the rest."},{"p":"Dad opened the trunk and discovered that somebody had left a muddy cleat on top of the grocery bags. He looked at me. I looked at Theo, who was already halfway across the parking lot."},{"p":"“Not mine,” I said."},{"p":"“It’s your size.”"},{"p":"“That’s incomplete information.”"},{"p":"Mom closed her eyes. “Lincoln.”"},{"p":"I picked up the cleat."},{"p":"The airplane kept going. We headed home."}]},{"t":"Ground School Notes — Weather Is Information, Not a Promise","blocks":[{"p":"Lincoln learned that the sky can look simple from a baseball field and complicated from a cockpit. These notes explain the real aviation ideas behind his third summer at Hype Teen Flight Academy. They’re a starting point for curious readers, not instructions for making actual flight decisions. Real pilots use current official weather products, approved procedures, aircraft information, and qualified instruction."},{"h":"1. What a METAR Actually Tells You"},{"p":"A METAR is an aviation weather report describing observed conditions at a particular location and time. It can include wind, visibility, significant weather, cloud layers, temperature, dew point, and altimeter setting. It describes observed conditions; it does not promise what the weather will be an hour later."},{"p":"A report from an airport twenty miles away might be useful, but it is not a window into every patch of sky between the two places. A thunderstorm, fog bank, or shower can affect one area while another remains clear."},{"p":"**Try it:** On a map, mark two towns near your home. Check ordinary public weather observations for both at approximately the same time. Are they identical? Why might they differ? Do this as a comparison activity on the ground, not as a flight-planning exercise."},{"h":"2. A Forecast Is a Forecast"},{"p":"A TAF, or Terminal Aerodrome Forecast, describes expected weather conditions for an airport during its stated valid period. Forecasters use evidence and experience, but weather changes. A forecast may be amended when new information calls for it."},{"p":"Here’s the distinction Lincoln kept forgetting: an observation tells you what was reported; a forecast tells you what is expected. Both need their time labels. A picture without a timestamp can be especially misleading."},{"p":"**Try it:** Write two sentences about tomorrow’s weather. Begin one with “I observed” and the other with “I predict.” Which one can you verify right now?"},{"h":"3. Ceiling and Visibility Aren’t the Same Thing"},{"p":"Visibility describes how far a person can see under the reported conditions. A ceiling, in aviation weather reporting, is the height above the ground of the lowest cloud layer reported as broken or overcast, or the vertical visibility into an obscuration. A few scattered clouds overhead do not automatically create a reported ceiling."},{"p":"Pilots need to understand both visibility and clouds. The requirements for visual flight depend on factors such as airspace, altitude, and time of day. It’s not enough to memorize one number and apply it everywhere."},{"p":"**Try it:** Sketch three skies: scattered clouds, a mostly cloud-covered sky, and thick fog. Label what you would want to know about visibility and clouds in each scene."},{"h":"4. Weather Radar Has a Clock"},{"p":"Radar can help show where precipitation is occurring, but a radar image may be several minutes old by the time someone sees it. Displayed images can be processed, delayed, or incomplete. A pilot must never treat a colorful picture as a real-time view through the windshield."},{"p":"Thunderstorms are especially hazardous to aircraft. They can bring turbulence, lightning, hail, heavy rain, and strong shifting winds. Pilots avoid them rather than trying to thread through a gap that merely looks safe on a screen."},{"p":"**Try it:** Look at a public weather animation with an adult. Pause it and find the timestamp. What might have changed between that image and the present?"},{"h":"5. Personal Minimums Are More Than Legal Minimums"},{"p":"Regulations establish requirements, but a flight can meet a legal threshold and still be a poor choice for a particular pilot, airplane, route, or situation. Pilots also consider experience, fatigue, equipment, terrain, and whether the conditions are changing."},{"p":"Personal minimums are conservative limits a pilot establishes before pressure builds. A more experienced pilot might be comfortable in conditions that would be inappropriate for a newer one. A student should make real flight decisions with the supervising instructor and within the applicable rules."},{"p":"**Try it:** Think of a non-aviation activity, such as biking in the rain. Write down three conditions that would make you change your plan even if you were technically allowed to go."},{"h":"6. The Alternative Must Be Real"},{"p":"“We’ll just go somewhere else” sounds like a plan, but a usable alternate needs its own careful evaluation. Weather, fuel, airport suitability, aircraft performance, and other factors matter. An airport that looks close on a map may not be a practical destination."},{"p":"Pilots also plan fuel according to the flight and applicable rules. A required fuel reserve is a minimum safeguard, not a goal to arrive with as little fuel as possible."},{"p":"**Try it:** Imagine a family road trip. Your first stop is closed, so you choose another. What do you need to check before driving there? Consider distance, opening hours, and fuel."},{"h":"7. A No-Go Decision Is Still a Decision"},{"p":"In the story, Lincoln wanted to fly on a day that later turned stormy. Staying on the ground was not a prediction that disaster would definitely happen. It was a decision based on the information available at the time."},{"p":"This is a useful idea far beyond aviation: a good process doesn’t guarantee a pleasant outcome, and a lucky outcome doesn’t prove a risky choice was wise. Review decisions by asking what information was available, what alternatives existed, and whether the risks were reasonable—not only by looking backward."},{"p":"**Try it:** Describe a time you changed your plans for a good reason. Did you still feel disappointed? Both feelings can be true."},{"h":"8. The Best Question May Be “What Don’t We Know?”"},{"p":"Aviation uses checklists, briefings, and conversations partly because people can overlook details. Asking a question is not a sign that someone lacks courage or intelligence. It is a way to uncover missing information before a small misunderstanding becomes a larger problem."},{"p":"Lincoln’s friendships taught him a related lesson. Having information about someone doesn’t automatically mean you have permission to share it. In a cockpit, important safety information needs to reach the right people. In a friendship, private information deserves care. Good judgment includes knowing the difference."},{"p":"**Try it:** With a friend, invent a fictional trip affected by uncertain weather. Each of you writes three questions you’d want answered before deciding whether to go. Compare your lists. Did either person notice something the other missed?"},{"h":"Before the Next Book"},{"p":"In Book Four, Lincoln will begin connecting weather judgment to longer trips: charts, checkpoints, course planning, and what to do when the route ahead no longer makes sense. For now, remember that no single report, instrument, photograph, or confident voice tells the whole story. Curious pilots keep checking, keep learning, and leave room to change their minds."},{"h":"AVIATION NOTE"},{"p":"Aviation procedures, regulations, publications, equipment, and airport information can change. This series is fiction and is not a substitute for flight instruction, current official publications, aircraft operating information, or the judgment of a qualified instructor or pilot in command. Readers interested in learning to fly should use current FAA materials and instruction appropriate to their training."},{"h":"HYPE TEEN FLIGHT ACADEMY"},{"p":"Hype Teen Flight Academy follows Lincoln Burgess and his friends as they grow from curious beginners into young aviators who understand that good flying begins long before takeoff. Across seven books, real aviation concepts become part of stories about friendship, judgment, preparation, setbacks, independence, leadership, and service."},{"p":"1. *Balance Before Control*"},{"p":"2. *Crosswind Decisions*"},{"p":"3. *Weather Minimums*"},{"p":"4. *Cross-Country*"},{"p":"5. *Solo*"},{"p":"6. *Pilot in Command*"},{"p":"7. *The Sky We Share*"}],"links":[{"a":"unit:weather","t":"Weather"},{"a":"unit:adm","t":"Decisions + Risk"}]}],"words":29946}); })();
